PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L12U2.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":12,"cefr":"C2","unit":2,"theme":"Knowledge and Truth","essential_question":"How do we know what we know, and how should we decide what to believe?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1","title":"It Is Essential That Every Claim Be Tested: The Mandative Subjunctive","objectives":["Students will be able to use the base-form subjunctive after verbs, adjectives and nouns of demand and recommendation.","Students will be able to form negative and passive subjunctives correctly.","I can write \"He insisted that every claim be tested.\"","I can write \"The judge demanded that the witness not leave the room.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام صيغة المصدر الاقتضائية بعد الأفعال والصفات والأسماء الدالة على الطلب والتوصية.","سيتمكن الطلاب من صياغة الصيغة الاقتضائية المنفية والمبنية للمجهول بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع أن أكتب He insisted that every claim be tested.","أستطيع أن أكتب The judge demanded that the witness not leave the room."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"empirical","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɛmˈpɪrɪkəl/","definition":"based on observation or experiment rather than theory","arabic":"تجريبي / قائم على الملاحظة","example":"The theory has strong empirical support.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS1-W01"},{"word":"skeptical","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈskɛptəkəl/","definition":"doubting that something is true until you see evidence","arabic":"متشكك","example":"Scientists are skeptical of claims without data.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS1-W02"},{"word":"gullible","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɡʌləbəl/","definition":"too ready to believe what people tell you","arabic":"ساذج / سريع التصديق","example":"Gullible readers often share fake stories.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS1-W03"},{"word":"fallible","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈfæləbəl/","definition":"able to make mistakes","arabic":"قابل للخطأ","example":"Even experts are fallible.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"The mandative subjunctive uses the base form of the verb (no -s, no past tense) for all persons after words of demand, recommendation, necessity or importance: He insisted that every researcher test the claim. (NOT (not: tests)) The doctor recommended that she rest. It is essential that the belief be supported. It is formal and very common in American English.\nVerbs: insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask, advise + that + subject + base form. Adjectives: essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary, advisable + that… Nouns: the recommendation / demand / requirement / proposal / insistence + that… Negative: not + base form: The judge demanded that the witness not leave. Passive: be + past participle: He insisted that every claim be tested.\nThe were-subjunctive appears in formal unreal conditions and wishes: If I were the editor, I would check every source. He speaks as if he were an expert. Remember the tense of the main verb does not change the subjunctive: She recommends / recommended / will recommend that he rest.\ninsist / demand / recommend / suggest / propose / require + that + subject + base form\nessential / vital / crucial / imperative / important + that + subject + base form\nnegative: that he not leave • passive: that it be tested • be: that she be present\nsame form for all persons and tenses (that he go, that they go)","examples":["Ibn al-Haytham insisted that every claim be tested.","He recommended that researchers examine their own assumptions.","It is essential that a belief be supported by good reasons.","Teachers propose that every student learn to question sources.","The judge demanded that the witness not leave the room.","It is vital that the mind remain open to new evidence.","The journal requires that every article be reviewed by two experts.","If I were a journalist, I would check every quotation."],"common_errors":["✗ He insisted that every researcher tests the claim. → ✓ He insisted that every researcher test the claim. (Base form – no -s.)","✗ It is essential that the results are checked. (formal) → ✓ It is essential that the results be checked. (Subjunctive be.)","✗ The judge demanded that the witness doesn't leave. → ✓ The judge demanded that the witness not leave. (not + base form.)","✗ She recommended that he took a break. → ✓ She recommended that he take a break. (Base form, even in the past.)"],"arabic":"صيغة الأمر الاقتضائي (mandative subjunctive) تستخدم المصدر دون to ودون -s ولجميع الضمائر بعد كلمات الطلب والتوصية والضرورة: He insisted that every researcher test the claim.\nالأفعال: insist وdemand وrecommend وsuggest وpropose وrequire، والصفات: essential وvital وcrucial وimperative، والأسماء: the recommendation that…\nالنفي: that he not leave، والمبني للمجهول: that every claim be tested، ولا يتغير الشكل بتغير زمن الفعل الرئيس.\nصيغة were تظهر في الشرط غير الحقيقي الرسمي: If I were the editor…"},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “It Is Essential That Every Claim Be Tested: The Mandative Subjunctive”. Let’s learn the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that; negative (that he not leave); passive (that every claim be tested); be (that she be present); nouns (the recommendation / demand / requirement that…); the were-subjunctive in formal unreal conditions (If I were…, as if it were…) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U2-LS1 | It Is Essential That Every Claim Be Tested: The Mandative Subjunctive","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “How Do We Know What We Know?”: More than a thousand years ago, the scholar Ibn al-Haytham, working in Cairo, transformed the way people studied light and vision. Rather than simply accepting the theories of earlier authorities, he insisted that every claim be tested through careful observation and experiment. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"More than a thousand years ago, the scholar Ibn al-Haytham, working in Cairo, transformed the way people studied light and vision. Rather than simply accepting the theories of earlier authorities, he insisted that every claim be tested through careful observation and experiment.","visual":"Image GV-L12-U2-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"The mandative subjunctive uses the base form of the verb (no -s, no past tense) for all persons after words of demand, recommendation, necessity or importance: He insisted that every researcher test the claim. (NOT (not: tests)) The doctor recommended that she rest. It is essential that the belief be supported. It is formal and very common in American English. Verbs: insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask, advise + that + subject + base form. Adjectives: essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary, advisable + that… Nouns: the recommendation / demand / requirement / proposal / insistence + that… Negative: not + base form: The judge demanded that the witness not leave. Passive: be + past participle: He insisted that every claim be tested.","on_screen":"insist / demand / recommend / suggest / propose / require + that + subject + base form • essential / vital / crucial / imperative / important + that + subject + base form • negative: that he not leave • passive: that it be tested • be: that she be present","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Ibn al-Haytham insisted that every claim be tested. He recommended that researchers examine their own assumptions. It is essential that a belief be supported by good reasons.","on_screen":"Ibn al-Haytham insisted that every claim be tested. / He recommended that researchers examine their own assumptions. / It is essential that a belief be supported by good reasons.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “He insisted that every researcher tests the claim.” That’s not right. We say: “He insisted that every researcher test the claim.” Base form – no -s.","on_screen":"✗ He insisted that every researcher tests the claim.   ✓ He insisted that every researcher test the claim.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"He insisted that every claim ___ tested.\" is or be? … Think! … *be*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"He insisted that every claim be tested.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"He insisted that every claim be tested.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that; negative (that he not leave); passive (that every claim be tested); be (that she be present); nouns (the recommendation / demand / requirement that…); the were-subjunctive in formal unreal conditions (If I were…, as if it were…).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","صيغة الأمر الاقتضائي (mandative subjunctive) تستخدم المصدر دون to ودون -s ولجميع الضمائر بعد كلمات الطلب والتوصية والضرورة: He insisted that every researcher test the claim.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: He insisted that every researcher tests the claim. — الصحيح: He insisted that every researcher test the claim.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب He insisted that every claim be tested."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"How Do We Know What We Know?","body":"More than a thousand years ago, the scholar Ibn al-Haytham, working in Cairo, transformed the way people studied light and vision. Rather than simply accepting the theories of earlier authorities, he insisted that every claim be tested through careful observation and experiment. He also recommended that researchers examine their own assumptions, since even the most respected scholars were fallible. For this reason, many historians regard him as a pioneer of the scientific method.\nHis advice is as relevant today as it was then. We are surrounded by information, yet distinguishing knowledge from opinion has rarely been more difficult. A 2018 study at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology found that false news stories spread faster and further on social media than true ones, partly because they tend to be more surprising.\nHow, then, should we decide what to believe? Philosophers have long argued that knowledge requires more than a belief that happens to be true; it is essential that the belief be supported by good reasons. Many teachers now propose that every student learn to ask three questions about any claim: Who says so? What is the evidence? Can it be checked by others?\nBe that as it may, skepticism has its limits. If we doubted everything, we could never act at all. Suffice it to say that a healthy mind is neither gullible nor dogmatic. It is vital that it remain open to new evidence, while demanding that extraordinary claims be supported by extraordinary proof.\nIbn al-Haytham could not have imagined the internet. Nevertheless, his method of observing, testing, questioning and revising remains one of humanity's most powerful tools for discovering the truth.","word_count":273,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Ibn al-Haytham insisted that every claim ___ through careful experiment.","options":["is tested","be tested","tested","was tested"],"answer":"be tested","explanation":"Passive subjunctive (that it be done).","explanation_ar":"المبني للمجهول."},{"q":"He recommended that each researcher ___ his own assumptions.","options":["examined","examine","examines","to examine"],"answer":"examine","explanation":"Verb + that + base-form subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الفعل + that + المصدر."},{"q":"It is essential that a belief ___ by good reasons.","options":["be supported","is supported","supports","was supported"],"answer":"be supported","explanation":"Adjective + that + subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الصفة + that."},{"q":"The judge demanded that the witness ___ the courtroom.","options":["not leaves","doesn't leave","didn't leave","not leave"],"answer":"not leave","explanation":"Negative subjunctive (that he not…).","explanation_ar":"النفي."},{"q":"If I ___ the editor, I would check every source twice.","options":["will be","am","were","had"],"answer":"were","explanation":"The were-subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"صيغة were."},{"q":"The journal's new policy requires that every article submitted for publication ______ by at least two independent experts before it is accepted.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["was reviewed","is reviewed","be reviewed","will be reviewed"],"answer":"be reviewed","explanation":"Passive subjunctive (that it be done).","explanation_ar":"المبني للمجهول."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with the subjunctive form of the verb. Word bank: be repeated, cite, remain","items":[{"q":"The teacher asked that every student ______ (cite) at least three sources.","options":[],"answer":"cite","explanation":"Verb + that + base-form subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الفعل + that + المصدر."},{"q":"It is vital that the mind ______ (remain) open to new evidence.","options":[],"answer":"remain","explanation":"Adjective + that + subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الصفة + that."},{"q":"The committee's recommendation that the study ______ (repeat – passive) was accepted.","options":[],"answer":"be repeated","explanation":"Noun + that + subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الاسم + that."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"She recommended that he took a short break from social media.","options":[],"answer":"She recommended that he take a short break from social media.","explanation":"took → take. Verb + that + base-form subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: took → take. الفعل + that + المصدر."},{"q":"It is imperative that the data are checked before publication.","options":[],"answer":"It is imperative that the data be checked before publication.","explanation":"are → be. Passive subjunctive (that it be done).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: are → be. المبني للمجهول."},{"q":"The coach insisted that the players don't speak to reporters.","options":[],"answer":"The coach insisted that the players not speak to reporters.","explanation":"don't → not. Negative subjunctive (that he not…).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: don't → not. النفي."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Make recommendations.","items":[{"q":"Write a rule for a school library with \"It is essential that…\".","options":[],"answer":"It is essential that every student return books within two weeks.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that; negative (that he not leave); passive (that every claim be tested); be (that she be present); nouns (the recommendation / demand / requirement that…); the were-subjunctive in formal unreal conditions (If I were…, as if it were…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a recommendation for social media users with \"I recommend that…\".","options":[],"answer":"I recommend that every user check the source before sharing a story.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that; negative (that he not leave); passive (that every claim be tested); be (that she be present); nouns (the recommendation / demand / requirement that…); the were-subjunctive in formal unreal conditions (If I were…, as if it were…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a demand with a negative subjunctive.","options":[],"answer":"The principal demanded that students not use phones during exams.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that; negative (that he not leave); passive (that every claim be tested); be (that she be present); nouns (the recommendation / demand / requirement that…); the were-subjunctive in formal unreal conditions (If I were…, as if it were…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a passive subjunctive with \"require that\".","options":[],"answer":"The university requires that all essays be submitted online.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that; negative (that he not leave); passive (that every claim be tested); be (that she be present); nouns (the recommendation / demand / requirement that…); the were-subjunctive in formal unreal conditions (If I were…, as if it were…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"the suggestion that…\".","options":[],"answer":"The suggestion that the debate be held online was popular.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that; negative (that he not leave); passive (that every claim be tested); be (that she be present); nouns (the recommendation / demand / requirement that…); the were-subjunctive in formal unreal conditions (If I were…, as if it were…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A fact-checking policy.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 rules for a school newspaper using the subjunctive.","options":[],"answer":"The editor requires that every article be checked by two students. It is essential that each quotation include the speaker's full name.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that; negative (that he not leave); passive (that every claim be tested); be (that she be present); nouns (the recommendation / demand / requirement that…); the were-subjunctive in formal unreal conditions (If I were…, as if it were…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"It is crucial that… not…\".","options":[],"answer":"It is crucial that reporters not publish rumors as facts.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that; negative (that he not leave); passive (that every claim be tested); be (that she be present); nouns (the recommendation / demand / requirement that…); the were-subjunctive in formal unreal conditions (If I were…, as if it were…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"If I were…\".","options":[],"answer":"If I were the editor, I would publish a correction box every week.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that; negative (that he not leave); passive (that every claim be tested); be (that she be present); nouns (the recommendation / demand / requirement that…); the were-subjunctive in formal unreal conditions (If I were…, as if it were…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Ball Toss Sentences","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["Students stand in a circle. The teacher says a cue word and throws the ball.","The catcher makes a sentence with the cue word and the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that; negative (that he not leave); passive (that every claim be tested); be (that she be present); nouns (the recommendation / demand / requirement that…); the were-subjunctive in formal unreal conditions (If I were…, as if it were…).","If it is correct, the catcher chooses the next cue word and throws the ball to a new student.","If it is not correct, classmates help fix it, and the same student tries again."],"data":[{"label":"insisted that every claim be tested"},{"label":"It is essential that it be supported"},{"label":"recommended that researchers examine"},{"label":"demanded that he not leave"},{"label":"Be that as it may"},{"label":"Suffice it to say"},{"label":"It is vital that it remain"},{"label":"It is important for students to learn"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The doctor suggested that she ___ more water.","options":["drank","drink","drinks","to drink"],"answer":"drink","explanation":"Verb + that + base-form subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الفعل + that + المصدر."},{"q":"It is crucial that every voter ___ informed about the candidates.","options":["be","being","is","was"],"answer":"be","explanation":"Adjective + that + subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الصفة + that."},{"q":"The committee proposed that the rules ___ next year.","options":["were changed","are changed","change by","be changed"],"answer":"be changed","explanation":"Passive subjunctive (that it be done).","explanation_ar":"المبني للمجهول."},{"q":"The school requires that students ___ phones in the classroom.","options":["not using","don't use","not use","didn't use"],"answer":"not use","explanation":"Negative subjunctive (that he not…).","explanation_ar":"النفي."},{"q":"The manager's insistence that everyone ___ on time surprised the staff.","options":["arrive","arrives","arrived","arriving"],"answer":"arrive","explanation":"Noun + that + subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الاسم + that."},{"q":"","options":["It is important that she be present at the meeting.","It is important that she is being present at the meeting.","It is important that she be presents at the meeting.","It is important that she will be present at the meeting."],"answer":"It is important that she be present at the meeting.","explanation":"Adjective + that + subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الصفة + that."},{"q":"","options":["He speaks as if he were be the only expert in the room.","He speaks as if he is being the only expert in the room.","He speaks as if he be the only expert in the room.","He speaks as if he were the only expert in the room."],"answer":"He speaks as if he were the only expert in the room.","explanation":"The were-subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"صيغة were."},{"q":"Health officials urged that anyone experiencing symptoms ______ at home and contact a doctor rather than going directly to the hospital.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["staying","stays","stay","stayed"],"answer":"stay","explanation":"Verb + that + base-form subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الفعل + that + المصدر."},{"q":"The scientists asked that their data ______ publicly available so that other researchers could check their conclusions.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["being made","be made","was made","is made"],"answer":"be made","explanation":"Passive subjunctive (that it be done).","explanation_ar":"المبني للمجهول."},{"q":"Why is \"test\" correct in \"He insisted that every researcher test the claim\"?","options":["Because \"researcher\" is plural.","After \"insist that\" (a demand), we use the base form for all persons.","Because the sentence is in the present.","It is a mistake; \"tests\" is correct."],"answer":"After \"insist that\" (a demand), we use the base form for all persons.","explanation":"Verb + that + base-form subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الفعل + that + المصدر."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"A medieval scholar's study in Cairo: a man examining light passing through a small hole into a dark room (camera obscura), manuscripts with diagrams of the eye; split with a modern fact-checking newsroom where journalists compare sources on screens. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A medieval scholar's study in Cairo: a man examining light passing through a small hole into a dark room (camera obscura), manuscripts with diagrams of the eye; split with a modern fact-checking newsroom where journalists compare sources on screens"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that; negative (that he not leave); passive (that every claim be tested); be (that she be present); nouns (the recommendation / demand / requirement that…); the were-subjunctive in formal unreal conditions (If I were…, as if it were…), with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows empirical (based on observation or experiment rather than theory); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing empirical: based on observation or experiment rather than theory"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows skeptical (doubting that something is true until you see evidence); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing skeptical: doubting that something is true until you see evidence"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows gullible (too ready to believe what people tell you); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing gullible: too ready to believe what people tell you"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows fallible (able to make mistakes); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing fallible: able to make mistakes"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Ball Toss Sentences”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Ball Toss Sentences"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS1-W01","script":"empirical. empirical. The theory has strong empirical support.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS1-W02","script":"skeptical. skeptical. Scientists are skeptical of claims without data.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS1-W03","script":"gullible. gullible. Gullible readers often share fake stories.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS1-W04","script":"fallible. fallible. Even experts are fallible.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS1-T1","script":"More than a thousand years ago, the scholar Ibn al-Haytham, working in Cairo, transformed the way people studied light and vision. Rather than simply accepting the theories of earlier authorities, he insisted that every claim be tested through careful observation and experiment. He also recommended that researchers examine their own assumptions, since even the most respected scholars were fallible. For this reason, many historians regard him as a pioneer of the scientific method. His advice is as relevant today as it was then. We are surrounded by information, yet distinguishing knowledge from opinion has rarely been more difficult. A 2018 study at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology found that false news stories spread faster and further on social media than true ones, partly because they tend to be more surprising. How, then, should we decide what to believe? Philosophers have long argued that knowledge requires more than a belief that happens to be true; it is essential that the belief be supported by good reasons. Many teachers now propose that every student learn to ask three questions about any claim: Who says so? What is the evidence? Can it be checked by others? Be that as it may, skepticism has its limits. If we doubted everything, we could never act at all. Suffice it to say that a healthy mind is neither gullible nor dogmatic. It is vital that it remain open to new evidence, while demanding that extraordinary claims be supported by extraordinary proof. Ibn al-Haytham could not have imagined the internet. Nevertheless, his method of observing, testing, questioning and revising remains one of humanity's most powerful tools for discovering the truth.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “It Is Essential That Every Claim Be Tested: The Mandative Subjunctive”. Let’s learn the mandative subjunctive (base form for all persons) after verbs (insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask) and adjectives (essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary) + that; negative (that he not leave); passive (that every claim be tested); be (that she be present); nouns (the recommendation / demand / requirement that…); the were-subjunctive in formal unreal conditions (If I were…, as if it were…) together. Listen to the start of “How Do We Know What We Know?”: More than a thousand years ago, the scholar Ibn al-Haytham, working in Cairo, transformed the way people studied light and vision. Rather than simply accepting the theories of earlier authorities, he insisted that every claim be tested through careful observation and experiment. Look at the green words. The mandative subjunctive uses the base form of the verb (no -s, no past tense) for all persons after words of demand, recommendation, necessity or importance: He insisted that every researcher test the claim. (NOT (not: tests)) The doctor recommended that she rest. It is essential that the belief be supported. It is formal and very common in American English. Verbs: insist, demand, recommend, suggest, propose, require, request, urge, ask, advise + that + subject + base form. Adjectives: essential, vital, crucial, imperative, important, necessary, advisable + that… Nouns: the recommendation / demand / requirement / proposal / insistence + that… Negative: not + base form: The judge demanded that the witness not leave. Passive: be + past participle: He insisted that every claim be tested. Here are some examples. Ibn al-Haytham insisted that every claim be tested. He recommended that researchers examine their own assumptions. It is essential that a belief be supported by good reasons. Be careful! Some learners say: “He insisted that every researcher tests the claim.” That’s not right. We say: “He insisted that every researcher test the claim.” Base form – no -s. Quick check: \"He insisted that every claim ___ tested.\" is or be? … Think! … *be*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"He insisted that every claim be tested.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2","title":"Be That As It May; Suffice It to Say: Formulaic Subjunctives and Alternatives","objectives":["Students will be able to use formulaic subjunctive expressions appropriately in formal speech and writing.","Students will be able to rephrase subjunctive sentences with should or for… to and distinguish the two meanings of insist and suggest.","I can use \"Be that as it may\" and \"Suffice it to say\" in a formal argument.","I can explain why \"She insisted that he was innocent\" uses \"was\", not \"be\"."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام العبارات الاقتضائية الثابتة بشكل مناسب في الحديث والكتابة الرسمية.","سيتمكن الطلاب من إعادة صياغة الجمل الاقتضائية بـ should أو for… to، والتمييز بين معنيي insist وsuggest.","أستطيع أن أستخدم Be that as it may وSuffice it to say في حجة رسمية.","أستطيع أن أشرح لماذا تستخدم She insisted that he was innocent الفعل was لا be."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"dogmatic","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/dɑɡˈmætɪk/","definition":"insisting that your beliefs are right and refusing to consider other ideas","arabic":"متعصّب لرأيه / دوغمائي","example":"A dogmatic thinker never changes his mind.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS2-W01"},{"word":"premise","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈprɛmɪs/","definition":"an idea on which an argument is based","arabic":"مقدّمة منطقية","example":"If the premise is false, the conclusion may be false too.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS2-W02"},{"word":"inference","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪnfɚəns/","definition":"a conclusion reached from evidence and reasoning","arabic":"استنتاج","example":"The detective's inference turned out to be correct.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS2-W03"},{"word":"paradox","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpɛrəˌdɑks/","definition":"a statement or situation that seems impossible because it contains two opposite facts","arabic":"مفارقة","example":"It is a paradox that more information can make us less informed.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Some fixed expressions keep an old subjunctive form. Be that as it may = even so (accepting a point but continuing): The evidence is limited. Be that as it may, the theory is worth testing. Suffice it to say = I don't need to say more than: Suffice it to say that the results were disappointing. Come what may = whatever happens. So be it = I accept it. Far be it from me to criticize, but… (polite introduction to criticism). If need be = if necessary. whether it be X or Y = whether it is X or Y.\nAlternatives to the mandative subjunctive: (1) should + base verb (very common in British English): It is essential that every claim should be tested. (2) for + object + to-infinitive: It is essential for every claim to be tested. (3) In informal American and British English, the indicative is sometimes used: It's important that he is there. In formal and SAT-style writing, prefer the subjunctive or should.\ninsist and suggest have two meanings. When they mean demand / recommend, use the subjunctive: She insisted that he apologize. When they mean say strongly that something is true or indicate, use a normal tense: She insisted that he was innocent. The evidence suggests that the theory is wrong. Ask: is it a demand or a statement of fact?\nBe that as it may • Suffice it to say • Come what may • Far be it from me to • If need be • whether it be\nthat + should + base verb (British alternative)\nIt is essential for + object + to-infinitive\ninsist / suggest = demand → subjunctive • = state / indicate → indicative","examples":["The evidence is limited. Be that as it may, the theory deserves attention.","Suffice it to say that the experiment did not go as planned.","Come what may, we will finish the investigation.","Far be it from me to criticize, but your source is unreliable.","We can repeat the experiment if need be.","All claims, whether it be in newspapers or online, should be checked.","It is essential for every researcher to record the results.","The data suggest that the theory is incorrect."],"common_errors":["✗ Be it that as it may, the theory is interesting. → ✓ Be that as it may, the theory is interesting. (Fixed expression.)","✗ Suffice to say it that the results were poor. → ✓ Suffice it to say that the results were poor. (Fixed word order.)","✗ She insisted that he be innocent. (She believed he was innocent.) → ✓ She insisted that he was innocent. (Statement → indicative.)","✗ It is essential for every student attends. → ✓ It is essential for every student to attend. (for + object + to-infinitive.)"],"arabic":"بعض العبارات الثابتة تحتفظ بالصيغة الاقتضائية القديمة: Be that as it may (ومع ذلك)، Suffice it to say (يكفي القول)، Come what may (مهما يحدث)، Far be it from me to (لا أقصد أن…)، If need be (عند الحاجة).\nبدائل الصيغة الاقتضائية: should + المصدر (شائعة في البريطانية)، وfor + مفعول + to + المصدر: It is essential for every claim to be tested.\ninsist وsuggest لهما معنيان: الطلب ← صيغة اقتضائية (insisted that he apologize)، والإخبار بحقيقة ← زمن عادي (insisted that he was innocent)."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Be That As It May; Suffice It to Say: Formulaic Subjunctives and Alternatives”. Let’s learn formulaic subjunctive expressions (Be that as it may; Suffice it to say; Come what may; So be it; Far be it from me to…; If need be; Heaven forbid; Long live…; whether it be… or…); alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: should + base verb (It is essential that he should attend – more common in British English), for + object + to-infinitive (It is essential for him to attend), and the indicative in informal style; insist / suggest with two meanings (demand → subjunctive; report a fact → indicative) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U2-LS2 | Be That As It May; Suffice It to Say: Formulaic Subjunctives and Alternatives","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: Come what may, we will publish the truth. Far be it from me to question your sources, but this website is not reliable. Is it necessary for every student to attend?","on_screen":"Come what may, we will publish the truth. | Far be it from me to question your sources, but this website is not reliable. | Is it necessary for every student to attend?","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Some fixed expressions keep an old subjunctive form. Be that as it may = even so (accepting a point but continuing): The evidence is limited. Be that as it may, the theory is worth testing. Suffice it to say = I don't need to say more than: Suffice it to say that the results were disappointing. Come what may = whatever happens. So be it = I accept it. Far be it from me to criticize, but… (polite introduction to criticism). If need be = if necessary. whether it be X or Y = whether it is X or Y. Alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: (1) should + base verb (very common in British English): It is essential that every claim should be tested. (2) for + object + to-infinitive: It is essential for every claim to be tested. (3) In informal American and British English, the indicative is sometimes used: It's important that he is there. In formal and SAT-style writing, prefer the subjunctive or should.","on_screen":"Be that as it may • Suffice it to say • Come what may • Far be it from me to • If need be • whether it be • that + should + base verb (British alternative) • It is essential for + object + to-infinitive","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The evidence is limited. Be that as it may, the theory deserves attention. Suffice it to say that the experiment did not go as planned. Come what may, we will finish the investigation.","on_screen":"The evidence is limited. Be that as it may, the theory deserves attention. / Suffice it to say that the experiment did not go as planned. / Come what may, we will finish the investigation.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Be it that as it may, the theory is interesting.” That’s not right. We say: “Be that as it may, the theory is interesting.” Fixed expression.","on_screen":"✗ Be it that as it may, the theory is interesting.   ✓ Be that as it may, the theory is interesting.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: What does \"Be that as it may\" mean? … Think! … Even so / nevertheless. Great job! Now you can say: I can use \"Be that as it may\" and \"Suffice it to say\" in a formal argument.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can use \"Be that as it may\" and \"Suffice it to say\" in a formal argument.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: formulaic subjunctive expressions (Be that as it may; Suffice it to say; Come what may; So be it; Far be it from me to…; If need be; Heaven forbid; Long live…; whether it be… or…); alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: should + base verb (It is essential that he should attend – more common in British English), for + object + to-infinitive (It is essential for him to attend), and the indicative in informal style; insist / suggest with two meanings (demand → subjunctive; report a fact → indicative).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","بعض العبارات الثابتة تحتفظ بالصيغة الاقتضائية القديمة: Be that as it may (ومع ذلك)، Suffice it to say (يكفي القول)، Come what may (مهما يحدث)، Far be it from me to (لا أقصد أن…)، If need be (عند الحاجة).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Be it that as it may, the theory is interesting. — الصحيح: Be that as it may, the theory is interesting.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أستخدم Be that as it may وSuffice it to say في حجة رسمية."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"The evidence is limited. ___, the theory deserves further study.","options":["May it be as that","As it may be that","Be that as it may","Be it as that may"],"answer":"Be that as it may","explanation":"Formulaic subjunctive expression.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الثابتة."},{"q":"___ that the experiment did not go as planned.","options":["Suffice saying","Suffice it to say","It suffices saying","Suffice to say it"],"answer":"Suffice it to say","explanation":"Formulaic subjunctive expression.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الثابتة."},{"q":"She insisted that her brother ___ innocent; she was sure he hadn't done it.","options":["were to be","be","should be","was"],"answer":"was","explanation":"insist / suggest: demand or statement?","explanation_ar":"معنيا insist وsuggest."},{"q":"It is essential ___ every researcher to record the results.","options":["for","of","that","to"],"answer":"for","explanation":"for + object + to-infinitive.","explanation_ar":"for… to."},{"q":"The data ___ that the original theory is incorrect.","options":["suggests to be","are suggesting being","suggest that it be","suggest"],"answer":"suggest","explanation":"insist / suggest: demand or statement?","explanation_ar":"معنيا insist وsuggest."},{"q":"The professor admitted that the new method had weaknesses. ______ she argued, it was still more reliable than any alternative available at the time.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["Suffice it to say,","Be that as it may,","Come what may,","So be it,"],"answer":"Be that as it may,","explanation":"Formulaic subjunctive expression.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الثابتة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Rewrite with the words in brackets.","items":[{"q":"It is essential that every claim be tested. (should)  (should alternative)","options":[],"answer":"It is essential that every claim should be tested.","explanation":"should + base verb alternative.","explanation_ar":"بديل should."},{"q":"It is vital that students learn to question sources. (for… to)  (for + object + to-infinitive)","options":[],"answer":"It is vital for students to learn to question sources.","explanation":"for + object + to-infinitive.","explanation_ar":"for… to."},{"q":"I don't want to criticize you, but this website is unreliable. (Far be it from me)  (formulaic subjunctive)","options":[],"answer":"Far be it from me to criticize you, but this website is unreliable.","explanation":"Formulaic subjunctive expression.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الثابتة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"Suffice to say it that the results were disappointing.","options":[],"answer":"Suffice it to say that the results were disappointing.","explanation":"Suffice to say it → Suffice it to say. Formulaic subjunctive expression.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Suffice to say it → Suffice it to say. العبارة الثابتة."},{"q":"The witness insisted that she be at home all evening. (a statement of fact)","options":[],"answer":"The witness insisted that she was at home all evening. (a statement of fact)","explanation":"be → was. insist / suggest: demand or statement?","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: be → was. معنيا insist وsuggest."},{"q":"It is important for every student attends the safety talk.","options":[],"answer":"It is important for every student to attend the safety talk.","explanation":"attends → to attend. for + object + to-infinitive.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: attends → to attend. for… to."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Use formal expressions.","items":[{"q":"Concede a point and continue with \"Be that as it may\".","options":[],"answer":"Social media spreads rumors quickly. Be that as it may, it also helps people share important news.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formulaic subjunctive expressions (Be that as it may; Suffice it to say; Come what may; So be it; Far be it from me to…; If need be; Heaven forbid; Long live…; whether it be… or…); alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: should + base verb (It is essential that he should attend – more common in British English), for + object + to-infinitive (It is essential for him to attend), and the indicative in informal style; insist / suggest with two meanings (demand → subjunctive; report a fact → indicative) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Summarize a bad experience with \"Suffice it to say…\".","options":[],"answer":"Suffice it to say that I will never trust that website again.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formulaic subjunctive expressions (Be that as it may; Suffice it to say; Come what may; So be it; Far be it from me to…; If need be; Heaven forbid; Long live…; whether it be… or…); alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: should + base verb (It is essential that he should attend – more common in British English), for + object + to-infinitive (It is essential for him to attend), and the indicative in informal style; insist / suggest with two meanings (demand → subjunctive; report a fact → indicative) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Make a promise with \"Come what may\".","options":[],"answer":"Come what may, I will check every fact in my essay.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formulaic subjunctive expressions (Be that as it may; Suffice it to say; Come what may; So be it; Far be it from me to…; If need be; Heaven forbid; Long live…; whether it be… or…); alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: should + base verb (It is essential that he should attend – more common in British English), for + object + to-infinitive (It is essential for him to attend), and the indicative in informal style; insist / suggest with two meanings (demand → subjunctive; report a fact → indicative) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Offer polite criticism with \"Far be it from me to…\".","options":[],"answer":"Far be it from me to judge, but that article seems biased.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formulaic subjunctive expressions (Be that as it may; Suffice it to say; Come what may; So be it; Far be it from me to…; If need be; Heaven forbid; Long live…; whether it be… or…); alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: should + base verb (It is essential that he should attend – more common in British English), for + object + to-infinitive (It is essential for him to attend), and the indicative in informal style; insist / suggest with two meanings (demand → subjunctive; report a fact → indicative) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"if need be\".","options":[],"answer":"We can stay late to finish the project if need be.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formulaic subjunctive expressions (Be that as it may; Suffice it to say; Come what may; So be it; Far be it from me to…; If need be; Heaven forbid; Long live…; whether it be… or…); alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: should + base verb (It is essential that he should attend – more common in British English), for + object + to-infinitive (It is essential for him to attend), and the indicative in informal style; insist / suggest with two meanings (demand → subjunctive; report a fact → indicative) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A debate statement.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences of a debate opening using two formulaic subjunctives.","options":[],"answer":"Some say that certainty is impossible. Be that as it may, we can still distinguish strong evidence from weak evidence.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formulaic subjunctive expressions (Be that as it may; Suffice it to say; Come what may; So be it; Far be it from me to…; If need be; Heaven forbid; Long live…; whether it be… or…); alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: should + base verb (It is essential that he should attend – more common in British English), for + object + to-infinitive (It is essential for him to attend), and the indicative in informal style; insist / suggest with two meanings (demand → subjunctive; report a fact → indicative) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence using \"insist\" with a demand and one using \"insist\" with a statement.","options":[],"answer":"The editor insisted that the article be rewritten. The writer insisted that his facts were correct.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formulaic subjunctive expressions (Be that as it may; Suffice it to say; Come what may; So be it; Far be it from me to…; If need be; Heaven forbid; Long live…; whether it be… or…); alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: should + base verb (It is essential that he should attend – more common in British English), for + object + to-infinitive (It is essential for him to attend), and the indicative in informal style; insist / suggest with two meanings (demand → subjunctive; report a fact → indicative) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"whether it be… or…\".","options":[],"answer":"Every claim, whether it be in a textbook or on social media, deserves scrutiny.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formulaic subjunctive expressions (Be that as it may; Suffice it to say; Come what may; So be it; Far be it from me to…; If need be; Heaven forbid; Long live…; whether it be… or…); alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: should + base verb (It is essential that he should attend – more common in British English), for + object + to-infinitive (It is essential for him to attend), and the indicative in informal style; insist / suggest with two meanings (demand → subjunctive; report a fact → indicative) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Dice Sentences","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["Turn over a cue card and roll the die.","Make a sentence with the cue in the form shown by the die.","The player on the left checks; a correct sentence wins the card.","The player with the most cards after 7 minutes wins."],"data":[{"cue":"he / insist / every claim / test (subjunctive passive)"},{"cue":"it / be / essential / that / the belief / support (passive)"},{"cue":"she / recommend / that / researchers / examine assumptions"},{"cue":"the judge / demand / that / he / not leave"},{"cue":"(formula) be that as it may"},{"cue":"(formula) suffice it to say"},{"cue":"it / be / vital / that / the mind / remain open"},{"cue":"it / be / important / for students / learn to question"},{"cue":"the committee / propose / that / the rule / change (passive)"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"___, we will finish the research before the deadline.","options":["May come what","Come may what","Come what may","What may come"],"answer":"Come what may","explanation":"Formulaic subjunctive expression.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الثابتة."},{"q":"___ to tell you how to do your job, but the figures look wrong.","options":["Far be it from me","Be far from me","Far it be from me","It be far from me"],"answer":"Far be it from me","explanation":"Formulaic subjunctive expression.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الثابتة."},{"q":"We can repeat the survey ___.","options":["if need be","if needs being","if need is","if be need"],"answer":"if need be","explanation":"Formulaic subjunctive expression.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الثابتة."},{"q":"It is necessary that the report ___ submitted by Friday. (British style)","options":["should is","should be","should being","should to be"],"answer":"should be","explanation":"should + base verb alternative.","explanation_ar":"بديل should."},{"q":"The results ___ that the treatment is effective.","options":["are indicate","indicates being","indicate that it be","indicate"],"answer":"indicate","explanation":"insist / suggest: demand or statement?","explanation_ar":"معنيا insist وsuggest."},{"q":"","options":["It is crucial for the samples to be stored at a low temperature.","It is crucial for the samples are stored at a low temperature.","It is crucial for the samples storing at a low temperature.","It is crucial for the samples be stored at a low temperature."],"answer":"It is crucial for the samples to be stored at a low temperature.","explanation":"for + object + to-infinitive.","explanation_ar":"for… to."},{"q":"","options":["The manager insisted that the employee to apologize to the customer.","The manager insisted that the employee apologizes to the customer yesterday.","The manager insisted that the employee apologizing to the customer.","The manager insisted that the employee apologize to the customer."],"answer":"The manager insisted that the employee apologize to the customer.","explanation":"insist / suggest: demand or statement?","explanation_ar":"معنيا insist وsuggest."},{"q":"The committee members disagreed strongly about the proposal, and the meeting lasted five hours. ______ that no decision was reached.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["It suffice to say","Suffice it to say","Suffices it saying","Suffice to say it"],"answer":"Suffice it to say","explanation":"Formulaic subjunctive expression.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الثابتة."},{"q":"Every piece of information, ______ a government report or a viral video, should be checked against reliable sources.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["whether it being","whether be it","be it whether","whether it be"],"answer":"whether it be","explanation":"Formulaic subjunctive expression.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الثابتة."},{"q":"In which sentence does \"suggest\" mean \"indicate\" (not \"recommend\")?","options":["She suggested that we leave early.","They suggested that the rules be changed.","The evidence suggests that the theory is wrong.","I suggest that he rest."],"answer":"The evidence suggests that the theory is wrong.","explanation":"insist / suggest: demand or statement?","explanation_ar":"معنيا insist وsuggest."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"A university debate on \"Can we ever be certain?\": a speaker at a podium with notes containing \"Be that as it may…\", \"Suffice it to say…\"; a moderator's card reading \"It is essential that each speaker be brief\"; students listening. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A university debate on \"Can we ever be certain?\": a speaker at a podium with notes containing \"Be that as it may…\", \"Suffice it to say…\"; a moderator's card reading \"It is essential that each speaker be brief\"; students listening"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of formulaic subjunctive expressions (Be that as it may; Suffice it to say; Come what may; So be it; Far be it from me to…; If need be; Heaven forbid; Long live…; whether it be… or…); alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: should + base verb (It is essential that he should attend – more common in British English), for + object + to-infinitive (It is essential for him to attend), and the indicative in informal style; insist / suggest with two meanings (demand → subjunctive; report a fact → indicative): a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Knowledge and Truth”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of formulaic subjunctive expressions (Be that as it may"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows dogmatic (insisting that your beliefs are right and refusing to consider other ideas); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing dogmatic: insisting that your beliefs are right and refusing to consider other ideas"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows premise (an idea on which an argument is based); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing premise: an idea on which an argument is based"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows inference (a conclusion reached from evidence and reasoning); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing inference: a conclusion reached from evidence and reasoning"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows paradox (a statement or situation that seems impossible because it contains two opposite facts); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing paradox: a statement or situation that seems impossible because it contains two opposite facts"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Dice Sentences”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Dice Sentences"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS2-W01","script":"dogmatic. dogmatic. A dogmatic thinker never changes his mind.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS2-W02","script":"premise. premise. If the premise is false, the conclusion may be false too.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS2-W03","script":"inference. inference. The detective's inference turned out to be correct.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS2-W04","script":"paradox. paradox. It is a paradox that more information can make us less informed.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Be That As It May; Suffice It to Say: Formulaic Subjunctives and Alternatives”. Let’s learn formulaic subjunctive expressions (Be that as it may; Suffice it to say; Come what may; So be it; Far be it from me to…; If need be; Heaven forbid; Long live…; whether it be… or…); alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: should + base verb (It is essential that he should attend – more common in British English), for + object + to-infinitive (It is essential for him to attend), and the indicative in informal style; insist / suggest with two meanings (demand → subjunctive; report a fact → indicative) together. Remember these patterns: Come what may, we will publish the truth. Far be it from me to question your sources, but this website is not reliable. Is it necessary for every student to attend? Some fixed expressions keep an old subjunctive form. Be that as it may = even so (accepting a point but continuing): The evidence is limited. Be that as it may, the theory is worth testing. Suffice it to say = I don't need to say more than: Suffice it to say that the results were disappointing. Come what may = whatever happens. So be it = I accept it. Far be it from me to criticize, but… (polite introduction to criticism). If need be = if necessary. whether it be X or Y = whether it is X or Y. Alternatives to the mandative subjunctive: (1) should + base verb (very common in British English): It is essential that every claim should be tested. (2) for + object + to-infinitive: It is essential for every claim to be tested. (3) In informal American and British English, the indicative is sometimes used: It's important that he is there. In formal and SAT-style writing, prefer the subjunctive or should. Here are some examples. The evidence is limited. Be that as it may, the theory deserves attention. Suffice it to say that the experiment did not go as planned. Come what may, we will finish the investigation. Be careful! Some learners say: “Be it that as it may, the theory is interesting.” That’s not right. We say: “Be that as it may, the theory is interesting.” Fixed expression. Quick check: What does \"Be that as it may\" mean? … Think! … Even so / nevertheless. Great job! Now you can say: I can use \"Be that as it may\" and \"Suffice it to say\" in a formal argument.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3","title":"Skeptical, Not Cynical; Confident, Not Arrogant: Knowledge Vocabulary and Connotation","objectives":["Students will be able to discuss knowledge and evidence with precise vocabulary.","Students will be able to choose words with the right connotation to express attitude.","I can explain the difference between empirical and anecdotal evidence.","I can say why \"skeptical\" is positive but \"cynical\" is negative."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من مناقشة المعرفة والأدلة بمفردات دقيقة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من اختيار الكلمات ذات الدلالة المناسبة للتعبير عن الموقف.","أستطيع أن أشرح الفرق بين الدليل التجريبي والدليل القائم على الروايات.","أستطيع أن أشرح لماذا skeptical إيجابية وcynical سلبية."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"corroborate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kɚˈɑbɚˌeɪt/","definition":"to support a claim with additional evidence","arabic":"يؤيّد / يعزّز بالدليل","example":"Two witnesses corroborated her story.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W01"},{"word":"anecdotal","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌænəkˈdoʊtəl/","definition":"based on personal stories rather than careful research","arabic":"قائم على الروايات الشخصية","example":"The evidence for the cure is only anecdotal.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W02"},{"word":"conjecture","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈdʒɛktʃɚ/","definition":"an opinion formed without complete information","arabic":"تخمين / حدس","example":"His explanation was pure conjecture.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W03"},{"word":"misconception","pos":"noun","ipa":"/mɪskənˈsɛpʃən/","definition":"a belief that is not correct","arabic":"فكرة خاطئة / مفهوم مغلوط","example":"It is a common misconception that we use only 10% of our brains.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W04"},{"word":"discern","pos":"verb","ipa":"/dɪˈsɝn/","definition":"to notice or understand something that is not obvious","arabic":"يميّز / يدرك","example":"It is hard to discern the truth online.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W05"},{"word":"assumption","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈsʌmpʃən/","definition":"something you believe is true without proof","arabic":"افتراض","example":"We must question our assumptions.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W06"},{"word":"scrutiny","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈskrutəni/","definition":"careful and detailed examination","arabic":"تدقيق / فحص دقيق","example":"The study did not survive close scrutiny.","image_id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W07","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W07"}],"explanation":{"text":"Evidence words: empirical evidence (from observation and experiment) vs anecdotal evidence (from personal stories); conjecture (an opinion without full information); a misconception (a wrong belief); to corroborate (support with more evidence); scrutiny (careful examination); to discern (notice what isn't obvious). Reasoning words: a premise (the starting idea of an argument), an inference (a conclusion from evidence), an assumption (something believed without proof), a paradox (two facts that seem to contradict each other).\nAttitude words: skeptical (doubting until there is evidence), gullible (too ready to believe), fallible (able to make mistakes), dogmatic (refusing to consider other ideas). Knowledge is a belief supported by good reasons; a belief may or may not be supported; an opinion is a personal view.\nConnotation is the feeling a word carries. Similar meaning, different attitude: skeptical (+, careful) vs cynical (–, believes nobody); confident (+) vs arrogant (–); open-minded (+) vs gullible (–); firm (+) vs stubborn (–); curious (+) vs nosy (–); questioning (+) vs suspicious (often –). Choose the word that matches your attitude: praising or criticizing.\nCollocations/phrases: empirical evidence • anecdotal evidence • corroborate a claim • close scrutiny | a false premise • draw an inference • challenge an assumption • pure conjecture | a common misconception • discern the truth • a healthy skepticism • a dogmatic attitude | skeptical / cynical • confident / arrogant • open-minded / gullible • firm / stubborn","examples":["The theory is supported by strong empirical evidence.","The evidence for the cure is only anecdotal.","Two independent studies corroborated the findings.","It is a common misconception that lightning never strikes the same place twice.","If the premise is false, the conclusion may be false too.","Scientists are skeptical of claims without data, but they are not cynical.","Gullible readers often share fake stories.","The study did not survive close scrutiny."],"common_errors":["✗ The evidence is anecdotic. → ✓ The evidence is anecdotal. (Adjective → anecdotal.)","✗ My friend believed the fake story; he is very skeptical. → ✓ My friend believed the fake story; he is very gullible. (Too ready to believe → gullible.)","✗ She is a cynical scientist who checks every result carefully. (praise) → ✓ She is a skeptical scientist who checks every result carefully. (Positive → skeptical.)","✗ The witness corroborated the story with no evidence. → ✓ The witness's account corroborated the story. / The story was corroborated by new evidence. (corroborate = support with evidence.)"],"arabic":"كلمات الأدلة: empirical (تجريبي) مقابل anecdotal (قائم على الروايات)، وconjecture (تخمين) وmisconception (فكرة خاطئة) وcorroborate (يعزّز بالدليل) وscrutiny (تدقيق).\nكلمات الاستدلال: premise (مقدّمة) وinference (استنتاج) وassumption (افتراض) وparadox (مفارقة).\nالدلالة: skeptical إيجابية (متحفّظ حتى يرى الدليل) وcynical سلبية (لا يثق بأحد)، وconfident إيجابية وarrogant سلبية، وopen-minded إيجابية وgullible سلبية.\nنختار الكلمة التي تناسب موقفنا: مدحًا أو نقدًا."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Skeptical, Not Cynical; Confident, Not Arrogant: Knowledge Vocabulary and Connotation”. Let’s learn epistemology vocabulary (empirical, skeptical, gullible, fallible, dogmatic, premise, inference, paradox, corroborate, anecdotal, conjecture, misconception, discern, assumption, scrutiny); knowledge vs belief vs opinion; connotation pairs (skeptical + / cynical –; confident + / arrogant –; open-minded + / gullible –; firm + / stubborn –; curious + / nosy –; questioning + / suspicious –); collocations (empirical evidence, a false premise, draw an inference, corroborate a claim, anecdotal evidence, pure conjecture, a common misconception, close scrutiny, challenge an assumption) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U2-LS3 | Skeptical, Not Cynical; Confident, Not Arrogant: Knowledge Vocabulary and Connotation","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. corroborate: to support a claim with additional evidence. anecdotal: based on personal stories rather than careful research. conjecture: an opinion formed without complete information.","on_screen":"corroborate | anecdotal | conjecture","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Evidence words: empirical evidence (from observation and experiment) vs anecdotal evidence (from personal stories); conjecture (an opinion without full information); a misconception (a wrong belief); to corroborate (support with more evidence); scrutiny (careful examination); to discern (notice what isn't obvious). Reasoning words: a premise (the starting idea of an argument), an inference (a conclusion from evidence), an assumption (something believed without proof), a paradox (two facts that seem to contradict each other). Attitude words: skeptical (doubting until there is evidence), gullible (too ready to believe), fallible (able to make mistakes), dogmatic (refusing to consider other ideas). Knowledge is a belief supported by good reasons; a belief may or may not be supported; an opinion is a personal view.","on_screen":"epistemology vocabulary (empirical, skeptical, gullible, fallible, dogmatic, premise, inference, paradox, corroborate, anecdotal, conjecture, misconception, discern, assumption, scrutiny); knowledge vs belief vs opinion; connotation pairs (skeptical + / cynical –; confident + / arrogant –; open-minded + / gullible –; firm + / stubborn –; curious + / nosy –; questioning + / suspicious –); collocations (empirical evidence, a false premise, draw an inference, corroborate a claim, anecdotal evidence, pure conjecture, a common misconception, close scrutiny, challenge an assumption)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The theory is supported by strong empirical evidence. The evidence for the cure is only anecdotal. Two independent studies corroborated the findings.","on_screen":"The theory is supported by strong empirical evidence. / The evidence for the cure is only anecdotal. / Two independent studies corroborated the findings.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The evidence is anecdotic.” That’s not right. We say: “The evidence is anecdotal.” Adjective → anecdotal.","on_screen":"✗ The evidence is anecdotic.   ✓ The evidence is anecdotal.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: Empirical or anecdotal: \"My uncle says this tea cured his cold\"? … Think! … *anecdotal*. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain the difference between empirical and anecdotal evidence.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can explain the difference between empirical and anecdotal evidence.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: epistemology vocabulary (empirical, skeptical, gullible, fallible, dogmatic, premise, inference, paradox, corroborate, anecdotal, conjecture, misconception, discern, assumption, scrutiny); knowledge vs belief vs opinion; connotation pairs (skeptical + / cynical –; confident + / arrogant –; open-minded + / gullible –; firm + / stubborn –; curious + / nosy –; questioning + / suspicious –); collocations (empirical evidence, a false premise, draw an inference, corroborate a claim, anecdotal evidence, pure conjecture, a common misconception, close scrutiny, challenge an assumption).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","كلمات الأدلة: empirical (تجريبي) مقابل anecdotal (قائم على الروايات)، وconjecture (تخمين) وmisconception (فكرة خاطئة) وcorroborate (يعزّز بالدليل) وscrutiny (تدقيق).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The evidence is anecdotic. — الصحيح: The evidence is anecdotal.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أشرح الفرق بين الدليل التجريبي والدليل القائم على الروايات."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","items":[{"q":"\"My uncle says this tea cured his cold\" is an example of ___ evidence.","options":["empirical","scientific","corroborated","anecdotal"],"answer":"anecdotal","explanation":"Knowledge word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Two independent studies ___ the original findings.","options":["discerned","conjectured","corroborated","assumed"],"answer":"corroborated","explanation":"Knowledge collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"My friend believed every fake story he read; he is very ___.","options":["dogmatic","fallible","gullible","skeptical"],"answer":"gullible","explanation":"Connotation (positive or negative).","explanation_ar":"الدلالة."},{"q":"She checks every result carefully before she accepts it; she is ___ in a healthy way.","options":["skeptical","gullible","nosy","cynical"],"answer":"skeptical","explanation":"Connotation (positive or negative).","explanation_ar":"الدلالة."},{"q":"It is a common ___ that we use only 10% of our brains.","options":["inference","misconception","premise","scrutiny"],"answer":"misconception","explanation":"Knowledge word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Because the researcher had relied on a single small survey, her conclusion was little more than ______, and other scientists called for a much larger study.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["conjecture","scrutiny","paradox","corroboration"],"answer":"conjecture","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the word to its meaning.","items":[{"q":"premise","options":["able to make mistakes","a conclusion reached from evidence","the idea on which an argument is based","careful and detailed examination","a situation that seems to contain two opposite truths","refusing to consider ideas other than your own"],"answer":"the idea on which an argument is based","explanation":"premise → the idea on which an argument is based. Knowledge word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"inference","options":["able to make mistakes","a conclusion reached from evidence","the idea on which an argument is based","careful and detailed examination","a situation that seems to contain two opposite truths","refusing to consider ideas other than your own"],"answer":"a conclusion reached from evidence","explanation":"inference → a conclusion reached from evidence. Knowledge word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"paradox","options":["able to make mistakes","a conclusion reached from evidence","the idea on which an argument is based","careful and detailed examination","a situation that seems to contain two opposite truths","refusing to consider ideas other than your own"],"answer":"a situation that seems to contain two opposite truths","explanation":"paradox → a situation that seems to contain two opposite truths. Knowledge word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"fallible","options":["able to make mistakes","a conclusion reached from evidence","the idea on which an argument is based","careful and detailed examination","a situation that seems to contain two opposite truths","refusing to consider ideas other than your own"],"answer":"able to make mistakes","explanation":"fallible → able to make mistakes. Knowledge word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"dogmatic","options":["able to make mistakes","a conclusion reached from evidence","the idea on which an argument is based","careful and detailed examination","a situation that seems to contain two opposite truths","refusing to consider ideas other than your own"],"answer":"refusing to consider ideas other than your own","explanation":"dogmatic → refusing to consider ideas other than your own. Knowledge word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"scrutiny","options":["able to make mistakes","a conclusion reached from evidence","the idea on which an argument is based","careful and detailed examination","a situation that seems to contain two opposite truths","refusing to consider ideas other than your own"],"answer":"careful and detailed examination","explanation":"scrutiny → careful and detailed examination. Knowledge word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Think critically.","items":[{"q":"Give an example of a common misconception.","options":[],"answer":"Many people think that camels store water in their humps, but they store fat.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses epistemology vocabulary (empirical, skeptical, gullible, fallible, dogmatic, premise, inference, paradox, corroborate, anecdotal, conjecture, misconception, discern, assumption, scrutiny); knowledge vs belief vs opinion; connotation pairs (skeptical + / cynical –; confident + / arrogant –; open-minded + / gullible –; firm + / stubborn –; curious + / nosy –; questioning + / suspicious –); collocations (empirical evidence, a false premise, draw an inference, corroborate a claim, anecdotal evidence, pure conjecture, a common misconception, close scrutiny, challenge an assumption) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe a time you were skeptical about something online.","options":[],"answer":"I was skeptical about a post claiming that a drink could cure diabetes.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses epistemology vocabulary (empirical, skeptical, gullible, fallible, dogmatic, premise, inference, paradox, corroborate, anecdotal, conjecture, misconception, discern, assumption, scrutiny); knowledge vs belief vs opinion; connotation pairs (skeptical + / cynical –; confident + / arrogant –; open-minded + / gullible –; firm + / stubborn –; curious + / nosy –; questioning + / suspicious –); collocations (empirical evidence, a false premise, draw an inference, corroborate a claim, anecdotal evidence, pure conjecture, a common misconception, close scrutiny, challenge an assumption) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Explain the difference between empirical and anecdotal evidence.","options":[],"answer":"Empirical evidence comes from experiments; anecdotal evidence comes from personal stories.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses epistemology vocabulary (empirical, skeptical, gullible, fallible, dogmatic, premise, inference, paradox, corroborate, anecdotal, conjecture, misconception, discern, assumption, scrutiny); knowledge vs belief vs opinion; connotation pairs (skeptical + / cynical –; confident + / arrogant –; open-minded + / gullible –; firm + / stubborn –; curious + / nosy –; questioning + / suspicious –); collocations (empirical evidence, a false premise, draw an inference, corroborate a claim, anecdotal evidence, pure conjecture, a common misconception, close scrutiny, challenge an assumption) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe a paradox you have noticed.","options":[],"answer":"It is a paradox that social media can make people feel lonely.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses epistemology vocabulary (empirical, skeptical, gullible, fallible, dogmatic, premise, inference, paradox, corroborate, anecdotal, conjecture, misconception, discern, assumption, scrutiny); knowledge vs belief vs opinion; connotation pairs (skeptical + / cynical –; confident + / arrogant –; open-minded + / gullible –; firm + / stubborn –; curious + / nosy –; questioning + / suspicious –); collocations (empirical evidence, a false premise, draw an inference, corroborate a claim, anecdotal evidence, pure conjecture, a common misconception, close scrutiny, challenge an assumption) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Rewrite with a positive word: \"My teacher is stubborn about deadlines.\"","options":[],"answer":"My teacher is firm about deadlines.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses epistemology vocabulary (empirical, skeptical, gullible, fallible, dogmatic, premise, inference, paradox, corroborate, anecdotal, conjecture, misconception, discern, assumption, scrutiny); knowledge vs belief vs opinion; connotation pairs (skeptical + / cynical –; confident + / arrogant –; open-minded + / gullible –; firm + / stubborn –; curious + / nosy –; questioning + / suspicious –); collocations (empirical evidence, a false premise, draw an inference, corroborate a claim, anecdotal evidence, pure conjecture, a common misconception, close scrutiny, challenge an assumption) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Analyze a claim.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences evaluating a viral claim using 3 knowledge words.","options":[],"answer":"The claim that onions cure the flu is based on anecdotal evidence. No empirical studies corroborate it. It should not survive close scrutiny.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses epistemology vocabulary (empirical, skeptical, gullible, fallible, dogmatic, premise, inference, paradox, corroborate, anecdotal, conjecture, misconception, discern, assumption, scrutiny); knowledge vs belief vs opinion; connotation pairs (skeptical + / cynical –; confident + / arrogant –; open-minded + / gullible –; firm + / stubborn –; curious + / nosy –; questioning + / suspicious –); collocations (empirical evidence, a false premise, draw an inference, corroborate a claim, anecdotal evidence, pure conjecture, a common misconception, close scrutiny, challenge an assumption) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences: one praising someone's attitude and one criticizing it, using a connotation pair.","options":[],"answer":"My brother is confident when he speaks in public. However, he sometimes sounds arrogant when he disagrees.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses epistemology vocabulary (empirical, skeptical, gullible, fallible, dogmatic, premise, inference, paradox, corroborate, anecdotal, conjecture, misconception, discern, assumption, scrutiny); knowledge vs belief vs opinion; connotation pairs (skeptical + / cynical –; confident + / arrogant –; open-minded + / gullible –; firm + / stubborn –; curious + / nosy –; questioning + / suspicious –); collocations (empirical evidence, a false premise, draw an inference, corroborate a claim, anecdotal evidence, pure conjecture, a common misconception, close scrutiny, challenge an assumption) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"challenge an assumption\".","options":[],"answer":"Good scientists challenge their own assumptions.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses epistemology vocabulary (empirical, skeptical, gullible, fallible, dogmatic, premise, inference, paradox, corroborate, anecdotal, conjecture, misconception, discern, assumption, scrutiny); knowledge vs belief vs opinion; connotation pairs (skeptical + / cynical –; confident + / arrogant –; open-minded + / gullible –; firm + / stubborn –; curious + / nosy –; questioning + / suspicious –); collocations (empirical evidence, a false premise, draw an inference, corroborate a claim, anecdotal evidence, pure conjecture, a common misconception, close scrutiny, challenge an assumption) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Vocabulary Bingo","mechanic":"matching","rules":["Students write nine words from the list in their grid.","The teacher (or a student caller) reads a definition or Arabic meaning.","Students cross out the matching word.","Three in a row = \"Bingo!\" The winner must read the words and say what each means."],"data":[{"word":"empirical","match":"based on observation or experiment rather than theory","arabic":"تجريبي / قائم على الملاحظة"},{"word":"skeptical","match":"doubting that something is true until you see evidence","arabic":"متشكك"},{"word":"gullible","match":"too ready to believe what people tell you","arabic":"ساذج / سريع التصديق"},{"word":"fallible","match":"able to make mistakes","arabic":"قابل للخطأ"},{"word":"dogmatic","match":"insisting that your beliefs are right and refusing to consider other ideas","arabic":"متعصّب لرأيه / دوغمائي"},{"word":"premise","match":"an idea on which an argument is based","arabic":"مقدّمة منطقية"},{"word":"inference","match":"a conclusion reached from evidence and reasoning","arabic":"استنتاج"},{"word":"paradox","match":"a statement or situation that seems impossible because it contains two opposite facts","arabic":"مفارقة"},{"word":"corroborate","match":"to support a claim with additional evidence","arabic":"يؤيّد / يعزّز بالدليل"},{"word":"anecdotal","match":"based on personal stories rather than careful research","arabic":"قائم على الروايات الشخصية"},{"word":"conjecture","match":"an opinion formed without complete information","arabic":"تخمين / حدس"},{"word":"misconception","match":"a belief that is not correct","arabic":"فكرة خاطئة / مفهوم مغلوط"},{"word":"discern","match":"to notice or understand something that is not obvious","arabic":"يميّز / يدرك"},{"word":"assumption","match":"something you believe is true without proof","arabic":"افتراض"},{"word":"scrutiny","match":"careful and detailed examination","arabic":"تدقيق / فحص دقيق"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The theory has strong ___ support from dozens of experiments.","options":["empirical","gullible","anecdotal","dogmatic"],"answer":"empirical","explanation":"Knowledge word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"If the ___ of an argument is false, the conclusion may also be false.","options":["premise","paradox","conjecture","scrutiny"],"answer":"premise","explanation":"Knowledge word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Even the greatest scientists are ___; they can make mistakes.","options":["infallible","dogmatic","gullible","fallible"],"answer":"fallible","explanation":"Knowledge word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"It is hard to ___ the truth when so many sources disagree.","options":["conjecture","assume","discern","corroborate"],"answer":"discern","explanation":"Knowledge collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"He refuses to listen to anyone who disagrees with him; he is completely ___.","options":["open-minded","dogmatic","curious","skeptical"],"answer":"dogmatic","explanation":"Connotation (positive or negative).","explanation_ar":"الدلالة."},{"q":"Which word criticizes someone who is sure of himself?","options":["self-assured","poised","confident","arrogant"],"answer":"arrogant","explanation":"Connotation (positive or negative).","explanation_ar":"الدلالة."},{"q":"The detective's ___ was that the thief knew the house well.","options":["premise","paradox","inference","misconception"],"answer":"inference","explanation":"Knowledge word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The new evidence came under close ___ from other scientists.","options":["conjecture","scrutiny","assumption","paradox"],"answer":"scrutiny","explanation":"Knowledge collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"The reviewer praised the author's ______ approach: rather than accepting popular claims about nutrition, she examined the research behind each one.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["gullible","skeptical","cynical","dogmatic"],"answer":"skeptical","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"It seems a ______ that, although people today have access to more information than ever before, many of them feel less certain about what is true.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["corroboration","scrutiny","premise","paradox"],"answer":"paradox","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"A \"Truth Detective\" classroom: cards on the wall with \"Empirical evidence\", \"Anecdotal evidence\", \"Conjecture\", \"Misconception\"; a scale balancing \"skeptical\" against \"cynical\"; students analyzing a viral health claim on a screen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A \"Truth Detective\" classroom: cards on the wall with \"Empirical evidence\", \"Anecdotal evidence\", \"Conjecture\", \"Misconception\"; a scale balancing \"skeptical\" against \"cynical\"; students analyzing a viral health claim on a screen"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows corroborate (to support a claim with additional evidence); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing corroborate: to support a claim with additional evidence"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows anecdotal (based on personal stories rather than careful research); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing anecdotal: based on personal stories rather than careful research"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows conjecture (an opinion formed without complete information); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing conjecture: an opinion formed without complete information"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows misconception (a belief that is not correct); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing misconception: a belief that is not correct"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows discern (to notice or understand something that is not obvious); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing discern: to notice or understand something that is not obvious"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows assumption (something you believe is true without proof); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing assumption: something you believe is true without proof"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-W07","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows scrutiny (careful and detailed examination); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing scrutiny: careful and detailed examination"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Vocabulary Bingo”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Vocabulary Bingo"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W01","script":"corroborate. corroborate. Two witnesses corroborated her story.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W02","script":"anecdotal. anecdotal. The evidence for the cure is only anecdotal.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W03","script":"conjecture. conjecture. His explanation was pure conjecture.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W04","script":"misconception. misconception. It is a common misconception that we use only 10% of our brains.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W05","script":"discern. discern. It is hard to discern the truth online.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W06","script":"assumption. assumption. We must question our assumptions.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-W07","script":"scrutiny. scrutiny. The study did not survive close scrutiny.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Skeptical, Not Cynical; Confident, Not Arrogant: Knowledge Vocabulary and Connotation”. Let’s learn epistemology vocabulary (empirical, skeptical, gullible, fallible, dogmatic, premise, inference, paradox, corroborate, anecdotal, conjecture, misconception, discern, assumption, scrutiny); knowledge vs belief vs opinion; connotation pairs (skeptical + / cynical –; confident + / arrogant –; open-minded + / gullible –; firm + / stubborn –; curious + / nosy –; questioning + / suspicious –); collocations (empirical evidence, a false premise, draw an inference, corroborate a claim, anecdotal evidence, pure conjecture, a common misconception, close scrutiny, challenge an assumption) together. Meet some new words. corroborate: to support a claim with additional evidence. anecdotal: based on personal stories rather than careful research. conjecture: an opinion formed without complete information. Evidence words: empirical evidence (from observation and experiment) vs anecdotal evidence (from personal stories); conjecture (an opinion without full information); a misconception (a wrong belief); to corroborate (support with more evidence); scrutiny (careful examination); to discern (notice what isn't obvious). Reasoning words: a premise (the starting idea of an argument), an inference (a conclusion from evidence), an assumption (something believed without proof), a paradox (two facts that seem to contradict each other). Attitude words: skeptical (doubting until there is evidence), gullible (too ready to believe), fallible (able to make mistakes), dogmatic (refusing to consider other ideas). Knowledge is a belief supported by good reasons; a belief may or may not be supported; an opinion is a personal view. Here are some examples. The theory is supported by strong empirical evidence. The evidence for the cure is only anecdotal. Two independent studies corroborated the findings. Be careful! Some learners say: “The evidence is anecdotic.” That’s not right. We say: “The evidence is anecdotal.” Adjective → anecdotal. Quick check: Empirical or anecdotal: \"My uncle says this tea cured his cold\"? … Think! … *anecdotal*. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain the difference between empirical and anecdotal evidence.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS4","title":"Credible or Credulous?: Precise Word Choice and Confusable Words (SAT)","objectives":["Students will be able to choose between near-synonyms according to connotation and context.","Students will be able to distinguish commonly confused words in academic writing.","I can choose between \"credible\" (believable) and \"credulous\" (too ready to believe).","I can explain why \"notorious\" is not the same as \"famous\"."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من الاختيار بين المترادفات المتقاربة حسب الدلالة والسياق.","سيتمكن الطلاب من التمييز بين الكلمات المتشابهة الشائعة في الكتابة الأكاديمية.","أستطيع أن أختار بين credible (جدير بالتصديق) وcredulous (سريع التصديق).","أستطيع أن أشرح لماذا notorious ليست مثل famous."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"empirical","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɛmˈpɪrɪkəl/","definition":"based on observation or experiment rather than theory","arabic":"تجريبي / قائم على الملاحظة","example":"The theory has strong empirical support.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"skeptical","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈskɛptəkəl/","definition":"doubting that something is true until you see evidence","arabic":"متشكك","example":"Scientists are skeptical of claims without data.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"gullible","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɡʌləbəl/","definition":"too ready to believe what people tell you","arabic":"ساذج / سريع التصديق","example":"Gullible readers often share fake stories.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"fallible","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈfæləbəl/","definition":"able to make mistakes","arabic":"قابل للخطأ","example":"Even experts are fallible.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"dogmatic","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/dɑɡˈmætɪk/","definition":"insisting that your beliefs are right and refusing to consider other ideas","arabic":"متعصّب لرأيه / دوغمائي","example":"A dogmatic thinker never changes his mind.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"premise","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈprɛmɪs/","definition":"an idea on which an argument is based","arabic":"مقدّمة منطقية","example":"If the premise is false, the conclusion may be false too.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Denotation is the dictionary meaning; connotation is the attitude or feeling. famous (neutral / +) vs notorious (famous for something bad); thrifty (+, careful with money) vs stingy (–, unwilling to spend); assertive (+, confident) vs aggressive (–, hostile); childlike (+, innocent) vs childish (–, immature); slim (+) vs skinny (often –). SAT asks for the most logical and precise word: it must fit the meaning and the tone of the context.\nConfusable pairs: credible (believable) / credulous (too ready to believe); compliment (praise) / complement (complete or go well with); principle (a rule or belief) / principal (main; the head of a school); eminent (famous and respected) / imminent (about to happen); discreet (careful, not drawing attention) / discrete (separate); elicit (draw out a response) / illicit (illegal).\nMore pairs: allude to (mention indirectly) / elude (escape, avoid); adverse (harmful: adverse effects) / averse (unwilling: averse to risk); continual (repeated, with breaks) / continuous (without stopping); disinterested (neutral, not taking sides) / uninterested (not interested); flout (break a rule openly) / flaunt (show off). Read the whole sentence before choosing: the context tells you which meaning is needed.\ndenotation = dictionary meaning • connotation = attitude\ncredible / credulous • compliment / complement • principle / principal\neminent / imminent • discreet / discrete • elicit / illicit • allude / elude\nadverse / averse • continual / continuous • disinterested / uninterested • flout / flaunt","examples":["The witness gave a credible account of events.","Credulous readers believed the story without checking it.","The spices complement the flavor of the rice.","Honesty is the most important principle in journalism.","An eminent scientist reviewed the study.","Heavy rain made a flood imminent.","The drug has no serious adverse effects.","A judge must be disinterested, not taking either side."],"common_errors":["✗ The story was so credulous that everyone believed it. → ✓ The story was so credible that everyone believed it. (Believable → credible.)","✗ The teacher complemented my essay. → ✓ The teacher complimented my essay. (Praise → compliment.)","✗ The storm is eminent; we must leave now. → ✓ The storm is imminent; we must leave now. (About to happen → imminent.)","✗ The students were disinterested in the lecture and fell asleep. → ✓ The students were uninterested in the lecture. (Not interested → uninterested.)"],"arabic":"الدلالة المعجمية (denotation) هي معنى القاموس، والدلالة الإيحائية (connotation) هي الموقف أو الشعور: famous محايدة أو إيجابية، وnotorious مشهور بالسوء.\nكلمات متشابهة: credible (جدير بالتصديق) / credulous (سريع التصديق)، compliment (مدح) / complement (يكمّل)، principle (مبدأ) / principal (رئيسي أو مدير المدرسة)، eminent (بارز) / imminent (وشيك).\nadverse (ضار) / averse (نافر من)، continual (متكرر بانقطاع) / continuous (متواصل)، disinterested (محايد) / uninterested (غير مهتم)، flout (يخالف علنًا) / flaunt (يتباهى).\nفي أسئلة SAT نختار الكلمة الأدق منطقًا ونبرةً في سياقها."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Credible or Credulous?: Precise Word Choice and Confusable Words (SAT)”. Let’s learn denotation and connotation in word choice (famous / notorious; thrifty / stingy; assertive / aggressive; childlike / childish; slim / skinny); commonly confused words (credible / credulous; compliment / complement; principle / principal; eminent / imminent; discreet / discrete; elicit / illicit; allude / elude; adverse / averse; continual / continuous; disinterested / uninterested; flout / flaunt); SAT \"most logical and precise word\" questions together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U2-LS4 | Credible or Credulous?: Precise Word Choice and Confusable Words (SAT)","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Denotation is the dictionary meaning; connotation is the attitude or feeling. famous (neutral / +) vs notorious (famous for something bad); thrifty (+, careful with money) vs stingy (–, unwilling to spend); assertive (+, confident) vs aggressive (–, hostile); childlike (+, innocent) vs childish (–, immature); slim (+) vs skinny (often –). SAT asks for the most logical and precise word: it must fit the meaning and the tone of the context.","on_screen":"denotation and connotation in word choice (famous / notorious; thrifty / stingy; assertive / aggressive; childlike / childish; slim / skinny); commonly confused words (credible / credulous; compliment / complement; principle / principal; eminent / imminent; discreet / discrete; elicit / illicit; allude / elude; adverse / averse; continual / continuous; disinterested / uninterested; flout / flaunt); SAT \"most logical and precise word\" questions","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Denotation is the dictionary meaning; connotation is the attitude or feeling. famous (neutral / +) vs notorious (famous for something bad); thrifty (+, careful with money) vs stingy (–, unwilling to spend); assertive (+, confident) vs aggressive (–, hostile); childlike (+, innocent) vs childish (–, immature); slim (+) vs skinny (often –). SAT asks for the most logical and precise word: it must fit the meaning and the tone of the context. Confusable pairs: credible (believable) / credulous (too ready to believe); compliment (praise) / complement (complete or go well with); principle (a rule or belief) / principal (main; the head of a school); eminent (famous and respected) / imminent (about to happen); discreet (careful, not drawing attention) / discrete (separate); elicit (draw out a response) / illicit (illegal).","on_screen":"denotation = dictionary meaning • connotation = attitude • credible / credulous • compliment / complement • principle / principal • eminent / imminent • discreet / discrete • elicit / illicit • allude / elude","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The witness gave a credible account of events. Credulous readers believed the story without checking it. The spices complement the flavor of the rice.","on_screen":"The witness gave a credible account of events. / Credulous readers believed the story without checking it. / The spices complement the flavor of the rice.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The story was so credulous that everyone believed it.” That’s not right. We say: “The story was so credible that everyone believed it.” Believable → credible.","on_screen":"✗ The story was so credulous that everyone believed it.   ✓ The story was so credible that everyone believed it.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: Credible or credulous: \"a person who believes everything\"? … Think! … *credulous*. Great job! Now you can say: I can choose between \"credible\" (believable) and \"credulous\" (too ready to believe).","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can choose between \"credible\" (believable) and \"credulous\" (too ready to believe).","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: denotation and connotation in word choice (famous / notorious; thrifty / stingy; assertive / aggressive; childlike / childish; slim / skinny); commonly confused words (credible / credulous; compliment / complement; principle / principal; eminent / imminent; discreet / discrete; elicit / illicit; allude / elude; adverse / averse; continual / continuous; disinterested / uninterested; flout / flaunt); SAT \"most logical and precise word\" questions.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","الدلالة المعجمية (denotation) هي معنى القاموس، والدلالة الإيحائية (connotation) هي الموقف أو الشعور: famous محايدة أو إيجابية، وnotorious مشهور بالسوء.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The story was so credulous that everyone believed it. — الصحيح: The story was so credible that everyone believed it.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أختار بين credible (جدير بالتصديق) وcredulous (سريع التصديق)."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Scientist Wins National Award (editing passage)","body":"An imminent Saudi scientist received a national award yesterday. In her speech, she alluded to the difficulties she had faced, and she thanked her school principle for his support. She said that good research depends on the principal of honesty. She also warned that credulous evidence must always be checked by other researchers. Reporters praised her for being discrete about her private life.","word_count":63,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","items":[{"q":"The witness gave a ___ account that the jury believed.","options":["creditable","creed","credulous","credible"],"answer":"credible","explanation":"credible / credulous; eminent / imminent; elicit / illicit.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 1."},{"q":"The teacher ___ my essay, saying it was the best in the class.","options":["compiled","completed","complemented","complimented"],"answer":"complimented","explanation":"compliment / complement; principle / principal; discreet / discrete.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 2."},{"q":"Dark clouds gathered, and a storm seemed ___.","options":["immanent","imminent","emanant","eminent"],"answer":"imminent","explanation":"credible / credulous; eminent / imminent; elicit / illicit.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 1."},{"q":"The new medicine has few ___ effects.","options":["adversely","averse","adverse","adversary"],"answer":"adverse","explanation":"adverse / averse; allude / elude; flout / flaunt.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 3."},{"q":"The bank robber became ___ for his daring crimes.","options":["notorious","celebrated","renowned","famous"],"answer":"notorious","explanation":"Connotation of near-synonyms.","explanation_ar":"دلالة المترادفات."},{"q":"To ensure a fair result, the competition organizers appointed a panel of ______ judges who had no connection to any of the schools taking part.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["interested","uninterested","disinterested","uninteresting"],"answer":"disinterested","explanation":"continual / continuous; disinterested / uninterested.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 4."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS4-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct word in brackets. Word bank: principle, elicit, continuous","items":[{"q":"Freedom of speech is a basic ______ (principle / principal) of the constitution.","options":[],"answer":"principle","explanation":"compliment / complement; principle / principal; discreet / discrete.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 2."},{"q":"The interviewer's questions were designed to ______ (elicit / illicit) honest answers.","options":[],"answer":"elicit","explanation":"credible / credulous; eminent / imminent; elicit / illicit.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 1."},{"q":"The hum of the refrigerator was ______ (continual / continuous); it never stopped.","options":[],"answer":"continuous","explanation":"continual / continuous; disinterested / uninterested.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 4."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"The saffron compliments the flavor of the rice perfectly.","options":[],"answer":"The saffron complements the flavor of the rice perfectly.","explanation":"compliments → complements. compliment / complement; principle / principal; discreet / discrete.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: compliments → complements. الكلمات المتشابهة 2."},{"q":"Many investors are adverse to risk during a recession.","options":[],"answer":"Many investors are averse to risk during a recession.","explanation":"adverse → averse. adverse / averse; allude / elude; flout / flaunt.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: adverse → averse. الكلمات المتشابهة 3."},{"q":"The thief managed to allude the police for three weeks.","options":[],"answer":"The thief managed to elude the police for three weeks.","explanation":"allude → elude. adverse / averse; allude / elude; flout / flaunt.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: allude → elude. الكلمات المتشابهة 3."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the news report.","items":[{"q":"An imminent Saudi scientist received a national award yesterday.","options":[],"answer":"An eminent Saudi scientist received a national award yesterday.","explanation":"imminent → eminent. Connotation of near-synonyms.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: imminent → eminent. دلالة المترادفات."},{"q":"In her speech, she alluded to the difficulties she had faced, and she thanked her school principle for his support.","options":[],"answer":"In her speech, she alluded to the difficulties she had faced, and she thanked her school principal for his support.","explanation":"principle → principal. Connotation of near-synonyms.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: principle → principal. دلالة المترادفات."},{"q":"She said that good research depends on the principal of honesty.","options":[],"answer":"She said that good research depends on the principle of honesty.","explanation":"principal → principle. Connotation of near-synonyms.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: principal → principle. دلالة المترادفات."},{"q":"She also warned that credulous evidence must always be checked by other researchers.","options":[],"answer":"She also warned that credible evidence must always be checked by other researchers.","explanation":"credulous → credible. Connotation of near-synonyms.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: credulous → credible. دلالة المترادفات."},{"q":"Reporters praised her for being discrete about her private life.","options":[],"answer":"Reporters praised her for being discreet about her private life.","explanation":"discrete → discreet. Connotation of near-synonyms.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: discrete → discreet. دلالة المترادفات."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write precise sentences.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with \"credible\" and one with \"credulous\".","options":[],"answer":"The scientist's explanation was credible. Credulous readers believed the rumor immediately.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses denotation and connotation in word choice (famous / notorious; thrifty / stingy; assertive / aggressive; childlike / childish; slim / skinny); commonly confused words (credible / credulous; compliment / complement; principle / principal; eminent / imminent; discreet / discrete; elicit / illicit; allude / elude; adverse / averse; continual / continuous; disinterested / uninterested; flout / flaunt); SAT \"most logical and precise word\" questions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"compliment\" and one with \"complement\".","options":[],"answer":"My mother complimented my cooking. Mint tea complements a heavy meal.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses denotation and connotation in word choice (famous / notorious; thrifty / stingy; assertive / aggressive; childlike / childish; slim / skinny); commonly confused words (credible / credulous; compliment / complement; principle / principal; eminent / imminent; discreet / discrete; elicit / illicit; allude / elude; adverse / averse; continual / continuous; disinterested / uninterested; flout / flaunt); SAT \"most logical and precise word\" questions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe someone positively and then negatively using a near-synonym pair.","options":[],"answer":"My grandfather is thrifty and never wastes money. His neighbor, however, is stingy and never shares.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses denotation and connotation in word choice (famous / notorious; thrifty / stingy; assertive / aggressive; childlike / childish; slim / skinny); commonly confused words (credible / credulous; compliment / complement; principle / principal; eminent / imminent; discreet / discrete; elicit / illicit; allude / elude; adverse / averse; continual / continuous; disinterested / uninterested; flout / flaunt); SAT \"most logical and precise word\" questions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Letter Ladder","mechanic":"word_builder","rules":["The leader chooses a word from the list and draws one blank line per letter.","Teams take turns guessing letters. Correct letters are written on the lines.","Each wrong guess removes one rung of the ladder (start with 6 rungs).","The team that completes the word scores 2 points and uses it in a sentence."],"data":[{"word":"empirical","clue":"based on observation or experiment rather than theory"},{"word":"skeptical","clue":"doubting that something is true until you see evidence"},{"word":"fallible","clue":"able to make mistakes"},{"word":"corroborate","clue":"to support a claim with additional evidence"},{"word":"misconception","clue":"a belief that is not correct"},{"word":"scrutiny","clue":"careful and detailed examination"},{"word":"gullible","clue":"too ready to believe what people tell you"},{"word":"dogmatic","clue":"insisting that your beliefs are right and refusing to consider other ideas"},{"word":"premise","clue":"an idea on which an argument is based"},{"word":"inference","clue":"a conclusion reached from evidence and reasoning"},{"word":"paradox","clue":"a statement or situation that seems impossible because it contains two opposite facts"},{"word":"anecdotal","clue":"based on personal stories rather than careful research"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"An ___ historian gave the opening lecture.","options":["imminent","eminent","emanant","eminently"],"answer":"eminent","explanation":"credible / credulous; eminent / imminent; elicit / illicit.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 1."},{"q":"The school ___ welcomed the visitors.","options":["principal","principled","principle","principality"],"answer":"principal","explanation":"compliment / complement; principle / principal; discreet / discrete.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 2."},{"q":"She was ___ about her plans and told no one.","options":["discrete","discretion","discreet","discreetly"],"answer":"discreet","explanation":"compliment / complement; principle / principal; discreet / discrete.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 2."},{"q":"The players ___ the rules by refusing to shake hands.","options":["floated","flouted","flaunted","flounced"],"answer":"flouted","explanation":"adverse / averse; allude / elude; flout / flaunt.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 3."},{"q":"There were ___ interruptions throughout the meeting, so we never finished.","options":["continually","continuing on","continual","continuous"],"answer":"continual","explanation":"continual / continuous; disinterested / uninterested.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 4."},{"q":"Which word criticizes someone who is careful with money?","options":["stingy","thrifty","economical","careful with money"],"answer":"stingy","explanation":"Connotation of near-synonyms.","explanation_ar":"دلالة المترادفات."},{"q":"","options":["The rich businessman liked to flout his expensive cars.","The rich businessman liked to flaunt his expensive cars.","The rich businessman liked to float his expensive cars.","The rich businessman liked to flaunting his expensive cars."],"answer":"The rich businessman liked to flaunt his expensive cars.","explanation":"adverse / averse; allude / elude; flout / flaunt.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 3."},{"q":"Although the young chess player was only ten, she showed a remarkably ______ confidence, calmly defending her moves against much older opponents.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["notorious","aggressive","childish","assertive"],"answer":"assertive","explanation":"Most logical and precise word (SAT).","explanation_ar":"الكلمة الأدق."},{"q":"The journalist was praised for refusing to publish the rumor until she had found ______ evidence from at least two independent sources.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["creditable","incredible","credulous","credible"],"answer":"credible","explanation":"Most logical and precise word (SAT).","explanation_ar":"الكلمة الأدق."},{"q":"The study divides the learning process into four ______ stages, each with its own goals and assessments.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["discreet","discretionary","discrete","indiscreet"],"answer":"discrete","explanation":"Most logical and precise word (SAT).","explanation_ar":"الكلمة الأدق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Knowledge and Truth” that shows denotation and connotation in word choice (famous / notorious; thrifty / stingy; assertive / aggressive; childlike / childish; slim / skinny); commonly confused words (credible / credulous; compliment / complement; principle / principal; eminent / imminent; discreet / discrete; elicit / illicit; allude / elude; adverse / averse; continual / continuous; disinterested / uninterested; flout / flaunt); SAT \"most logical and precise word\" questions in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Knowledge and Truth” that shows denotation and connotation in word choice (famous / notorious; thrifty / stingy; assertive / aggressive; childlike / childish; slim / skinny); commonly confused words (credible / credulous; compliment / complement; principle / principal; eminent / imminent; discreet / discrete; elicit / illicit; allude / elude; adverse / averse; continual / continuous; disinterested / uninterested; flout / flaunt); SAT \"most logical and precise word\" questions in action"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Letter Ladder”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Letter Ladder"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Credible or Credulous?: Precise Word Choice and Confusable Words (SAT)”. Let’s learn denotation and connotation in word choice (famous / notorious; thrifty / stingy; assertive / aggressive; childlike / childish; slim / skinny); commonly confused words (credible / credulous; compliment / complement; principle / principal; eminent / imminent; discreet / discrete; elicit / illicit; allude / elude; adverse / averse; continual / continuous; disinterested / uninterested; flout / flaunt); SAT \"most logical and precise word\" questions together. Denotation is the dictionary meaning; connotation is the attitude or feeling. famous (neutral / +) vs notorious (famous for something bad); thrifty (+, careful with money) vs stingy (–, unwilling to spend); assertive (+, confident) vs aggressive (–, hostile); childlike (+, innocent) vs childish (–, immature); slim (+) vs skinny (often –). SAT asks for the most logical and precise word: it must fit the meaning and the tone of the context. Denotation is the dictionary meaning; connotation is the attitude or feeling. famous (neutral / +) vs notorious (famous for something bad); thrifty (+, careful with money) vs stingy (–, unwilling to spend); assertive (+, confident) vs aggressive (–, hostile); childlike (+, innocent) vs childish (–, immature); slim (+) vs skinny (often –). SAT asks for the most logical and precise word: it must fit the meaning and the tone of the context. Confusable pairs: credible (believable) / credulous (too ready to believe); compliment (praise) / complement (complete or go well with); principle (a rule or belief) / principal (main; the head of a school); eminent (famous and respected) / imminent (about to happen); discreet (careful, not drawing attention) / discrete (separate); elicit (draw out a response) / illicit (illegal). Here are some examples. The witness gave a credible account of events. Credulous readers believed the story without checking it. The spices complement the flavor of the rice. Be careful! Some learners say: “The story was so credulous that everyone believed it.” That’s not right. We say: “The story was so credible that everyone believed it.” Believable → credible. Quick check: Credible or credulous: \"a person who believes everything\"? … Think! … *credulous*. Great job! Now you can say: I can choose between \"credible\" (believable) and \"credulous\" (too ready to believe).","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS5","title":"How Should We Decide What to Believe?: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to propose guidelines for evaluating information in a formal discussion.","Students will be able to write a formal argumentative essay with subjunctives, precise vocabulary and careful connotation, and answer SAT-style transition and synthesis items.","I can propose rules for checking information.","I can write a formal argumentative essay about knowledge and truth."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من اقتراح إرشادات لتقييم المعلومات في نقاش رسمي.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة مقال حجاجي رسمي بالصيغة الاقتضائية والمفردات الدقيقة والدلالات المناسبة، والإجابة عن أسئلة الروابط والتركيب بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أقترح قواعد للتحقق من المعلومات.","أستطيع أن أكتب مقالًا حجاجيًّا رسميًّا عن المعرفة والحقيقة."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"empirical","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɛmˈpɪrɪkəl/","definition":"based on observation or experiment rather than theory","arabic":"تجريبي / قائم على الملاحظة","example":"The theory has strong empirical support.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"skeptical","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈskɛptəkəl/","definition":"doubting that something is true until you see evidence","arabic":"متشكك","example":"Scientists are skeptical of claims without data.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"gullible","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɡʌləbəl/","definition":"too ready to believe what people tell you","arabic":"ساذج / سريع التصديق","example":"Gullible readers often share fake stories.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"fallible","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈfæləbəl/","definition":"able to make mistakes","arabic":"قابل للخطأ","example":"Even experts are fallible.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"dogmatic","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/dɑɡˈmætɪk/","definition":"insisting that your beliefs are right and refusing to consider other ideas","arabic":"متعصّب لرأيه / دوغمائي","example":"A dogmatic thinker never changes his mind.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"premise","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈprɛmɪs/","definition":"an idea on which an argument is based","arabic":"مقدّمة منطقية","example":"If the premise is false, the conclusion may be false too.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"inference","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪnfɚəns/","definition":"a conclusion reached from evidence and reasoning","arabic":"استنتاج","example":"The detective's inference turned out to be correct.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"paradox","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpɛrəˌdɑks/","definition":"a statement or situation that seems impossible because it contains two opposite facts","arabic":"مفارقة","example":"It is a paradox that more information can make us less informed.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"corroborate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kɚˈɑbɚˌeɪt/","definition":"to support a claim with additional evidence","arabic":"يؤيّد / يعزّز بالدليل","example":"Two witnesses corroborated her story.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"anecdotal","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌænəkˈdoʊtəl/","definition":"based on personal stories rather than careful research","arabic":"قائم على الروايات الشخصية","example":"The evidence for the cure is only anecdotal.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"conjecture","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈdʒɛktʃɚ/","definition":"an opinion formed without complete information","arabic":"تخمين / حدس","example":"His explanation was pure conjecture.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"misconception","pos":"noun","ipa":"/mɪskənˈsɛpʃən/","definition":"a belief that is not correct","arabic":"فكرة خاطئة / مفهوم مغلوط","example":"It is a common misconception that we use only 10% of our brains.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"discern","pos":"verb","ipa":"/dɪˈsɝn/","definition":"to notice or understand something that is not obvious","arabic":"يميّز / يدرك","example":"It is hard to discern the truth online.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"assumption","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈsʌmpʃən/","definition":"something you believe is true without proof","arabic":"افتراض","example":"We must question our assumptions.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"scrutiny","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈskrutəni/","definition":"careful and detailed examination","arabic":"تدقيق / فحص دقيق","example":"The study did not survive close scrutiny.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 2: the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, and precise word choice.\nA formal argumentative essay states a clear thesis, gives reasons supported by evidence, answers a counterargument and concludes with a strong statement. Subjunctive structures make recommendations sound formal and authoritative; precise words show careful thinking.\n✔ It is essential that every reader be skeptical • require that media literacy be taught\n✔ Be that as it may • Suffice it to say\n✔ corroborated, credible, empirical, anecdotal, misconception\n✔ skeptical, not cynical • credible, not credulous","examples":["It is essential that every reader be skeptical.","I recommend that students check their sources.","Schools should require that media literacy be taught.","Be that as it may, a story is not proof.","Suffice it to say that careful thinkers are harder to mislead.","Readers should be skeptical, not cynical.","Look for claims that are corroborated by credible sources.","However, the cost of misinformation is far greater."],"common_errors":["✗ It is essential that every reader is skeptical. (formal) → ✓ It is essential that every reader be skeptical. (Subjunctive.)","✗ Schools should require that media literacy is teach. → ✓ Schools should require that media literacy be taught. (Passive subjunctive.)","✗ Look for credulous sources. → ✓ Look for credible sources. (Believable → credible.)"],"arabic":"نراجع صيغة الأمر الاقتضائي والعبارات الثابتة وبدائلها ومفردات المعرفة والدلالة واختيار الكلمة الدقيقة.\nالمقال الحجاجي الرسمي يعرض أطروحة واضحة، ويقدم أسبابًا مدعومة بالأدلة، ويرد على حجة مضادة، ويختم بعبارة قوية.\nالتراكيب الاقتضائية تجعل التوصيات رسمية وموثوقة، والكلمات الدقيقة تُظهر التفكير الحذر."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “How Should We Decide What to Believe?: Language in Use”. Let’s learn the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, precise word choice; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U2-LS5 | How Should We Decide What to Believe?: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Truth guidelines: in groups, write five rules for evaluating information online. Each rule must use a subjunctive structure (It is essential that…, We recommend that…, The school requires that…). Present your rules with one formulaic expression (Be that as it may, Suffice it to say…) and two knowledge words.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 2: the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, and precise word choice. A formal argumentative essay states a clear thesis, gives reasons supported by evidence, answers a counterargument and concludes with a strong statement. Subjunctive structures make recommendations sound formal and authoritative; precise words show careful thinking.","on_screen":"✔ It is essential that every reader be skeptical • require that media literacy be taught • ✔ Be that as it may • Suffice it to say • ✔ corroborated, credible, empirical, anecdotal, misconception","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. It is essential that every reader be skeptical. I recommend that students check their sources. Schools should require that media literacy be taught.","on_screen":"It is essential that every reader be skeptical. / I recommend that students check their sources. / Schools should require that media literacy be taught.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “It is essential that every reader is skeptical. (formal).” That’s not right. We say: “It is essential that every reader be skeptical.” Subjunctive.","on_screen":"✗ It is essential that every reader is skeptical. (formal)   ✓ It is essential that every reader be skeptical.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “Schools should require that media literacy is teach.”? … Think! … It should be “Schools should require that media literacy be taught.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can propose rules for checking information.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can propose rules for checking information.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, precise word choice; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع صيغة الأمر الاقتضائي والعبارات الثابتة وبدائلها ومفردات المعرفة والدلالة واختيار الكلمة الدقيقة.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: It is essential that every reader is skeptical. (formal) — الصحيح: It is essential that every reader be skeptical.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أقترح قواعد للتحقق من المعلومات."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Truth Detectives – Yusuf","body":"Every day, young people see hundreds of claims online, and many of them are false.\nIt is essential that every reader be skeptical, though not cynical, about what they see.\nFirst, I recommend that students check whether a claim is corroborated by credible sources.\nSecond, it is vital that they distinguish empirical evidence from anecdotal stories.\nA personal story may be moving; be that as it may, it is not proof.\nThird, schools should require that media literacy be taught in every grade.\nSome people argue that this would take time away from other subjects. However, the cost of misinformation is far greater.\nSuffice it to say that a society of careful thinkers is harder to mislead.","word_count":117,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Yusuf's essay. True or False?","items":[{"q":"Yusuf says young people see many false claims online.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Truth Detectives – Yusuf”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"He believes readers should be cynical about everything.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Truth Detectives – Yusuf”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"He recommends checking whether claims are corroborated by credible sources.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Truth Detectives – Yusuf”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"According to Yusuf, a personal story is strong proof.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Truth Detectives – Yusuf”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"He thinks media literacy should be taught in every grade.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Truth Detectives – Yusuf”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Yusuf ignores the argument that media literacy takes time from other subjects.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Truth Detectives – Yusuf”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"It is essential that every reader ___ skeptical about what they see.","options":["is","be","was","being"],"answer":"be","explanation":"Subjunctive or formulaic expression.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الاقتضائية."},{"q":"Schools should require that media literacy ___ in every grade.","options":["be taught","is taught","was taught","teaches"],"answer":"be taught","explanation":"Subjunctive or formulaic expression.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الاقتضائية."},{"q":"A personal story may be moving; ___, it is not proof.","options":["come what may","suffice it to say","so be it","be that as it may"],"answer":"be that as it may","explanation":"Subjunctive or formulaic expression.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الاقتضائية."},{"q":"Students should check whether a claim is ___ by credible sources.","options":["corroborated","assumed","conjectured","discerned"],"answer":"corroborated","explanation":"Knowledge vocabulary or precise word.","explanation_ar":"المفردات الدقيقة."},{"q":"Readers should be skeptical, not ___.","options":["thoughtful","careful","cynical","questioning"],"answer":"cynical","explanation":"Knowledge vocabulary or precise word.","explanation_ar":"المفردات الدقيقة."},{"q":"Personal stories can make an argument more memorable. ______ they should never replace empirical evidence in a scientific debate.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Therefore,","For example,","Similarly,","Nevertheless,"],"answer":"Nevertheless,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Write your truth guidelines.","items":[{"q":"Rule 1 with \"It is essential that…\": ___","options":[],"answer":"It is essential that readers check the date of every article.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, precise word choice; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Rule 2 with \"We recommend that…\": ___","options":[],"answer":"We recommend that students compare at least two sources.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, precise word choice; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Rule 3 with a negative subjunctive: ___","options":[],"answer":"We ask that students not share stories they have not checked.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, precise word choice; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A formulaic expression: ___","options":[],"answer":"Suffice it to say that one unchecked post can spread to millions of people.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, precise word choice; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Two knowledge words in one sentence: ___","options":[],"answer":"Anecdotal evidence should be treated with healthy skepticism.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, precise word choice; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your argumentative essay.","items":[{"q":"Plan: thesis, three reasons, counterargument, response, conclusion.","options":[],"answer":"Thesis: teach evaluation skills; reasons: misinformation spreads fast, anecdotes mislead, credible sources exist; counter: adults should decide; response: young people need tools; conclusion: careful thinkers","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, precise word choice; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your argumentative essay in response to the question (8–9 sentences).","options":[],"answer":"In a world full of information, it is vital that young people learn to discern truth from falsehood. First, it is essential that every claim be checked against credible sources. A post shared by thousands of people is not necessarily true; popularity is not proof. Second, I recommend that students distinguish empirical evidence from anecdotal stories. Be that as it may, they should not become cynical and reject every source. Some argue that adults should decide what young people read. However, adults are fallible too, and young people need their own tools. Suffice it to say that a skeptical, well-informed generation will be much harder to mislead.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, precise word choice; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: subjunctive base forms (be, check, not share)? formulaic expressions in the correct order? connotation of skeptical / cynical? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"It is essential that every claim is checked\" to \"It is essential that every claim be checked\".","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, precise word choice; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Treasure Hunt Review","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["Teams move to a station every 90 seconds.","At each station, write one correct sentence with the cue word and the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, precise word choice; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","Teams collect a \"treasure letter\" for each correct sentence.","Unscramble the letters at the end to reveal the secret word."],"data":[{"cue":"empirical","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"skeptical","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"gullible","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"fallible","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"dogmatic","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"premise","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"inference","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"paradox","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"corroborate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"anecdotal","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"conjecture","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"misconception","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"discern","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"assumption","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"scrutiny","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The editor insisted that the article ___ before publication.","options":["was corrected","is corrected","corrects","be corrected"],"answer":"be corrected","explanation":"Subjunctive or formulaic expression.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الاقتضائية."},{"q":"___ that the rumor spread to every school in the city.","options":["It suffice to say","Suffice it to say","Suffice to say it","Sufficing it to say"],"answer":"Suffice it to say","explanation":"Subjunctive or formulaic expression.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الاقتضائية."},{"q":"The report was based only on ___ evidence from a few interviews.","options":["empirical","corroborated","anecdotal","rigorous"],"answer":"anecdotal","explanation":"Knowledge vocabulary or precise word.","explanation_ar":"المفردات الدقيقة."},{"q":"She praised the speaker's ___ argument, which everyone found believable.","options":["credited","credible","creditable","credulous"],"answer":"credible","explanation":"Knowledge vocabulary or precise word.","explanation_ar":"المفردات الدقيقة."},{"q":"","options":["We recommend that students not share unverified stories.","We recommend that students not to share unverified stories.","We recommend that students don't share unverified stories yesterday.","We recommend that students not sharing unverified stories."],"answer":"We recommend that students not share unverified stories.","explanation":"Subjunctive or formulaic expression.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الاقتضائية."},{"q":"Many people assume that information on official-looking websites must be accurate. ______ some of these websites are created specifically to spread false claims.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Similarly,","For this reason,","Therefore,","In reality,"],"answer":"In reality,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"The researchers repeated the experiment three times. ______ they invited an independent laboratory to check their results.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Instead,","Nevertheless,","In addition,","However,"],"answer":"In addition,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Ibn al-Haytham was a scholar who worked in Cairo about a thousand years ago.\n• He studied light and vision.\n• He insisted that claims be tested by observation and experiment.\n• Many historians regard him as a pioneer of the scientific method.\n\nThe student wants to explain why Ibn al-Haytham is regarded as a pioneer of the scientific method. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Because he insisted that claims be tested by observation and experiment, Ibn al-Haytham is regarded by many historians as a pioneer of the scientific method.","He studied light and vision.","Ibn al-Haytham worked in Cairo.","Many historians study the past."],"answer":"Because he insisted that claims be tested by observation and experiment, Ibn al-Haytham is regarded by many historians as a pioneer of the scientific method.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• A 2018 study at MIT examined how news spreads on social media.\n• It found that false stories spread faster and further than true ones.\n• False stories tended to be more surprising.\n• Surprising stories are more likely to be shared.\n\nThe student wants to explain why false stories spread faster than true ones. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["False stories spread faster than true ones partly because they tend to be more surprising, and surprising stories are more likely to be shared.","Many people share stories.","A 2018 study was carried out at MIT.","The study examined social media."],"answer":"False stories spread faster than true ones partly because they tend to be more surprising, and surprising stories are more likely to be shared.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Pick the best thesis statement for a formal argumentative essay:","options":["Young people should be cynical about absolutely everything they read!","It is essential that young people are learning to tell credulous sources.","Suffice to say it, fake news is bad.","It is essential that young people learn to distinguish credible evidence from mere conjecture."],"answer":"It is essential that young people learn to distinguish credible evidence from mere conjecture.","explanation":"Subjunctive or formulaic expression.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الاقتضائية."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"A \"Media Literacy Week\" assembly: Yusuf presenting \"Five Rules for Truth Detectives\"; a screen with a viral post marked \"Claim\", \"Source\", \"Evidence\", \"Corroboration\"; students holding cards \"Skeptical, not cynical\". Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A \"Media Literacy Week\" assembly: Yusuf presenting \"Five Rules for Truth Detectives\"; a screen with a viral post marked \"Claim\", \"Source\", \"Evidence\", \"Corroboration\"; students holding cards \"Skeptical, not cynical\""},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Knowledge and Truth”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Knowledge and Truth”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L12-U2-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Treasure Hunt Review”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Treasure Hunt Review"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS5-T1","script":"Every day, young people see hundreds of claims online, and many of them are false. It is essential that every reader be skeptical, though not cynical, about what they see. First, I recommend that students check whether a claim is corroborated by credible sources. Second, it is vital that they distinguish empirical evidence from anecdotal stories. A personal story may be moving; be that as it may, it is not proof. Third, schools should require that media literacy be taught in every grade. Some people argue that this would take time away from other subjects. However, the cost of misinformation is far greater. Suffice it to say that a society of careful thinkers is harder to mislead.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U2-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “How Should We Decide What to Believe?: Language in Use”. Let’s learn the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, precise word choice; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Truth guidelines: in groups, write five rules for evaluating information online. Each rule must use a subjunctive structure (It is essential that…, We recommend that…, The school requires that…). Present your rules with one formulaic expression (Be that as it may, Suffice it to say…) and two knowledge words. Today you use all of Unit 2: the mandative subjunctive, formulaic subjunctives and alternatives, knowledge vocabulary and connotation, and precise word choice. A formal argumentative essay states a clear thesis, gives reasons supported by evidence, answers a counterargument and concludes with a strong statement. Subjunctive structures make recommendations sound formal and authoritative; precise words show careful thinking. Here are some examples. It is essential that every reader be skeptical. I recommend that students check their sources. Schools should require that media literacy be taught. Be careful! Some learners say: “It is essential that every reader is skeptical. (formal).” That’s not right. We say: “It is essential that every reader be skeptical.” Subjunctive. Quick check: what is wrong with “Schools should require that media literacy is teach.”? … Think! … It should be “Schools should require that media literacy be taught.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can propose rules for checking information.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"The professor insisted that every student ___ the original sources.","options":["read it to","reads","read","to read"],"answer":"read","explanation":"Verb + that + base-form subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الفعل + that + المصدر."},{"q":"It is vital that the results ___ by an independent laboratory.","options":["are checked","were checked","be checked","checks"],"answer":"be checked","explanation":"Passive subjunctive (that it be done).","explanation_ar":"المبني للمجهول."},{"q":"The coach demanded that the players ___ to the press.","options":["not speaking","not speak","didn't speak","don't speak"],"answer":"not speak","explanation":"Negative subjunctive (that he not…).","explanation_ar":"النفي."},{"q":"The committee's requirement that every member ___ a report was strictly enforced.","options":["submitting","submit","submits","submitted"],"answer":"submit","explanation":"Noun + that + subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الاسم + that."},{"q":"If I ___ a journalist, I would always name my sources.","options":["were","will be","being","am"],"answer":"were","explanation":"The were-subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"صيغة were."},{"q":"Medical guidelines recommend that anyone who has been bitten by a snake ______ calm and seek medical help immediately rather than trying to remove the venom.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["remained","remaining","remains","remain"],"answer":"remain","explanation":"Verb + that + base-form subjunctive.","explanation_ar":"الفعل + that + المصدر."},{"q":"The evidence is incomplete. ___, the theory is worth investigating.","options":["May be that as it","Be it as may that","Be that as it may","As it may be that"],"answer":"Be that as it may","explanation":"Formulaic subjunctive expression.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الثابتة."},{"q":"___ that the meeting did not go well.","options":["It sufficed saying","Suffices to saying","Suffice to say it","Suffice it to say"],"answer":"Suffice it to say","explanation":"Formulaic subjunctive expression.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الثابتة."},{"q":"It is essential ___ every researcher to keep accurate records.","options":["for","with","that","of"],"answer":"for","explanation":"for + object + to-infinitive.","explanation_ar":"for… to."},{"q":"The fingerprints ___ that the suspect was at the scene.","options":["suggest that he be","suggest","are suggest","suggests to be"],"answer":"suggest","explanation":"insist / suggest: demand or statement?","explanation_ar":"معنيا insist وsuggest."},{"q":"The witness insisted that she ______ at home all evening, although the security cameras appeared to show otherwise.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["had been","should be","be","were to be"],"answer":"had been","explanation":"insist / suggest: demand or statement?","explanation_ar":"معنيا insist وsuggest."},{"q":"\"My grandmother says this remedy works\" is ___ evidence.","options":["corroborated","empirical","anecdotal","experimental"],"answer":"anecdotal","explanation":"Knowledge word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Two further studies ___ the original results.","options":["conjectured","corroborated","assumed","discerned"],"answer":"corroborated","explanation":"Knowledge collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"She believes everything she reads online; she is far too ___.","options":["dogmatic","fallible","gullible","skeptical"],"answer":"gullible","explanation":"Connotation (positive or negative).","explanation_ar":"الدلالة."},{"q":"The historian warned that the popular story about the city's founding was based on a ______: in fact, no written records from that period support it.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["misconception","scrutiny","premise","corroboration"],"answer":"misconception","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"Which word has a positive connotation?","options":["pig-headed","stubborn","obstinate","firm"],"answer":"firm","explanation":"Connotation (positive or negative).","explanation_ar":"الدلالة."},{"q":"The witness gave a ___ account that the court accepted.","options":["credible","creed","credulous","credulity"],"answer":"credible","explanation":"credible / credulous; eminent / imminent; elicit / illicit.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 1."},{"q":"The red scarf ___ her dark dress perfectly.","options":["complements","completes up","complies","compliments"],"answer":"complements","explanation":"compliment / complement; principle / principal; discreet / discrete.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 2."},{"q":"Many people are ______ to change, preferring familiar routines even when new methods are clearly better.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["adverse","adversary","averted","averse"],"answer":"averse","explanation":"adverse / averse; allude / elude; flout / flaunt.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 3."},{"q":"Scientists are trained to doubt claims that lack evidence. ______ they are not cynical; they accept new ideas once the evidence is strong.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["For example,","Therefore,","However,","As a result,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Empirical evidence comes from observation and experiment.\n• Anecdotal evidence comes from personal stories.\n• Personal stories can be memorable and persuasive.\n• However, they may not represent most people's experiences.\n\nThe student wants to explain why anecdotal evidence is weaker than empirical evidence. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Personal stories are memorable.","Although personal stories can be persuasive, anecdotal evidence is weaker than empirical evidence because it may not represent most people's experiences.","Many people tell stories.","Empirical evidence comes from experiments."],"answer":"Although personal stories can be persuasive, anecdotal evidence is weaker than empirical evidence because it may not represent most people's experiences.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"it is essential that every claim is check by credulous sources.\"","options":["It is essential that every claim is checked by credible sources.","it is essential that every claim be checked by credible sources.","It is essential that every claim be checked by credulous sources.","It is essential that every claim be checked by credible sources."],"answer":"It is essential that every claim be checked by credible sources.","explanation":"Passive subjunctive (that it be done).","explanation_ar":"المبني للمجهول."},{"q":"Which sentence uses \"insist\" to make a demand?","options":["The writer insisted that his story was true.","He insisted that the shop was closed.","She insisted that she had locked the door.","The editor insisted that the story be rewritten."],"answer":"The editor insisted that the story be rewritten.","explanation":"insist / suggest: demand or statement?","explanation_ar":"معنيا insist وsuggest."},{"q":"Which pair is correctly matched?","options":["eminent = about to happen","imminent = about to happen","eminent = illegal","imminent = famous and respected"],"answer":"imminent = about to happen","explanation":"credible / credulous; eminent / imminent; elicit / illicit.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة 1."}],"theme_ar":"المعرفة والحقيقة","essential_question_ar":"كيف نعرف ما نعرفه، وكيف ينبغي أن نقرّر ما نصدّقه؟","can_do":"I can use the mandative subjunctive (It is essential that every claim be tested), use formulaic subjunctives and alternatives with should and for… to, discuss knowledge and evidence with precise vocabulary and connotation, and choose precisely between near-synonyms and confusable words.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أستخدم صيغة الأمر الاقتضائي (It is essential that every claim be tested)، وأستخدم العبارات الثابتة بالصيغة الاقتضائية وبدائلها مع should وfor… to، وأناقش المعرفة والأدلة بمفردات دقيقة ودلالات مناسبة، وأختار بدقة بين المترادفات المتقاربة والكلمات المتشابهة."});
